WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:08.240
So this is the second
lecture on data.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:11.300
In the last lecture, we talked
about different data sets

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:13.890
that were used to train
various language models.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:17.330
We did a historical
overview from the data sets

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:21.650
that were used to train BERT
to all the way up to HOMO

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:23.580
and everything in between.

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:26.090
And one of the things
I wanted to emphasize

00:00:26.090 --> 00:00:28.590
is that data doesn't
just fall from the sky.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:30.960
It exists often
in live services.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:33.990
They have to be explicitly
crawled or dumped.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:36.810
And then there's a bunch of
processing that has to happen.

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:40.070
And the processing involves
converting the raw HTML

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:43.010
into text, doing all sorts
of quality and language

00:00:43.010 --> 00:00:46.010
and toxicity, filtering,
deduplication, and so on,

00:00:46.010 --> 00:00:47.100
and so forth.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.750
So in this lecture, I would like
to take a deep dive into some

00:00:50.750 --> 00:00:54.800
of the mechanics on how quality
filtering and deduplication work

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:55.860
in particular.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.070
So first, we're going to
look at different algorithms

00:00:58.070 --> 00:01:01.480
for filtering, which
mostly are model based.

00:01:01.480 --> 00:01:03.930
So you train a classifier
or train some sort of model

00:01:03.930 --> 00:01:04.959
to filter.

00:01:04.959 --> 00:01:07.140
Then we're going to show
that this primitive can

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:09.967
be used for all sorts of
different types of filtering.

00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:12.300
And then finally, we're going
to look at some algorithms

00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:14.850
for deduplication.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.770
So this lecture will be
a little bit different

00:01:16.770 --> 00:01:21.420
in that it's going to focus
a lot on classical big data

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:22.840
processing algorithms.

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:27.180
And there'll be some fun math
in there for some of you.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:28.000
OK.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.330
So let's get started with
filtering algorithms.

00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:35.070
So the basic, high
level picture here

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:40.740
is that you're given
some target data, which

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:42.400
you don't have very much of it.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.790
Suppose it's data
that's high quality,

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:46.870
and you have a lot of raw data.

00:01:46.870 --> 00:01:48.760
So imagine, this
is common crawl.

00:01:48.760 --> 00:01:52.410
And your goal is you want to
find a subset of that raw data

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:56.620
to call it T prime,
which is similar to T

00:01:56.620 --> 00:02:00.040
So this is the pattern that you
should recognize in basically

00:02:00.040 --> 00:02:03.970
any filtering pipeline.

00:02:03.970 --> 00:02:07.040
And what do you want for
this filtering algorithm,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.130
you want it obviously
to generalize

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:12.380
from T. You don't want--
you already have T.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.650
So there's no point in getting
exactly the same data T.

00:02:15.650 --> 00:02:18.010
So there has to be
some generalization.

00:02:18.010 --> 00:02:20.600
And also has to
be extremely fast.

00:02:20.600 --> 00:02:24.170
So you have to run it on
all of the web, for example.

00:02:24.170 --> 00:02:26.170
So if this is
running a huge model,

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:30.043
that's going to be-- might
be as expensive as training.

00:02:30.043 --> 00:02:31.460
So you definitely
don't want that.

00:02:31.460 --> 00:02:34.660
Otherwise, you
might as well train.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:37.600
So we're going to look
at three different ways

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:44.320
that you can implement this
high level abstract primitive.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.420
So we'll start with
training an n gram model.

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.930
So this is something that
came up in the last lecture.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:54.928
So you can train an n gram
model with Kneser-Ney smoothing.

00:02:54.928 --> 00:02:56.470
You can use other
forms of smoothing,

00:02:56.470 --> 00:03:00.520
but Kneser-Ney has been
inherited from the.

00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:06.180
N-gram kind of modeling
statistical language processing

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:07.530
era.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:09.450
And there's a particular
implementation

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.020
that people tend to use in
this space called KenLM, which

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:14.760
is open source,
originally developed

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:18.120
for machine translation,
which implements Kneser-Neys

00:03:18.120 --> 00:03:18.670
smoothing.

00:03:18.670 --> 00:03:21.600
So you can go and
read about Kneser-Ney.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.720
It's pretty interesting,
but we're not

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.050
going to talk too much
about the details here.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:31.830
So this happens to be very
popular in data filtering.

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.060
There's no particular reason
it has to be this one,

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:36.480
but this is just
what people use.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.850
And the nice thing about n-gram
modeling is it's very simple.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:45.570
When I say fitting a model,
you should think of this

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.980
as just counting the number of
n-grams, and then normalizing.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.550
So just to go into a
little bit of depth here.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:56.920
So your starting point is
maximum likelihood estimation

00:03:56.920 --> 00:03:58.100
of language models.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.040
So you have a corpus
of text, and you

00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.930
want to estimate
conditional probabilities,

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:06.650
like p of n given the last
n minus 1 words, the cat.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:11.170
And what you do is you
count the number of n grams,

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.820
and then divide it by the
number of the n minus 1 grams

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.769
of the conditioning context.

00:04:17.769 --> 00:04:23.150
So this is very, very
easy in principle.

00:04:23.150 --> 00:04:26.980
Now, implementing this
efficiently takes a bit of work.

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:29.570
But the main problem is
that there's sparse counts.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:32.020
So many of the
n-grams are exactly

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.910
show up zero times even though
they're very reasonable,

00:04:34.910 --> 00:04:36.820
especially when n grows.

00:04:36.820 --> 00:04:40.330
And this was the whole point why
n gram models couldn't really

00:04:40.330 --> 00:04:42.520
be scaled appropriately
because you hit

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:44.890
the curse of dimensionality.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:52.120
So to mitigate this, some people
devised Kneser-Ney smoothing

00:04:52.120 --> 00:04:53.900
to handle unseen n-grams.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:55.380
And there's more
details on this,

00:04:55.380 --> 00:04:59.300
but roughly, this
says, well, maybe

00:04:59.300 --> 00:05:02.040
if I don't have enough
data to support this count,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:04.680
I can use a lower order n-gram.

00:05:04.680 --> 00:05:07.280
So remove one word
from the context

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.030
and count the number of
times in, appears given cat,

00:05:11.030 --> 00:05:16.580
and then try to either
interpolate or back off to that.

00:05:16.580 --> 00:05:17.580
OK.

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:23.090
So that's all I'll say about
how n-gram modeling works.

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:26.570
I think even in this era
of neural language model,

00:05:26.570 --> 00:05:29.520
it's good to know how
n-gram models work.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.200
So let's download the n-gram.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.980
So this is a model that's
been trained on Wikipedia.

00:05:37.980 --> 00:05:40.070
I'm just going to download it.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:44.910
And then let's punch
some sentences through.

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:49.460
So this sentence is Stanford
University was founded in 1885.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:52.350
This is actually an excerpt
taken from Wikipedia.

00:05:52.350 --> 00:05:55.860
So it should be reasonable.

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.060
And if you compute
the probability,

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:02.830
so you first compute
the log prob,

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:04.530
and then you compute
the perplexity

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:10.910
by this normalization,
then you get 187.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:11.410
OK.

00:06:11.410 --> 00:06:14.940
So it's some number.

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.210
And then here's another example.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:22.800
This is taken from
the CS 336 website.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:24.700
So let's see how well it does.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:28.300
So this is a higher perplexity,
means that it's less likely.

00:06:28.300 --> 00:06:30.900
Kind of makes sense because
this stuff doesn't really

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.680
probably show up in Wikipedia.

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.740
It's not in-- it's
not abnormally high

00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:37.770
because it's still fairly
good well-formed English.

00:06:37.770 --> 00:06:43.410
And many n-grams there, I think,
show up in Wikipedia as well.

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:44.680
What about ASDF?

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:50.940
This is just gibberish that
gets assigned higher perplexity.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:52.660
And then there's da, da, da, da.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:56.000
So this should get assigned
pretty high perplexity.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.470
But for some reason,
this particular n-gram

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:01.850
assigns a pretty low perplexity.

00:07:01.850 --> 00:07:08.110
So it's an n-gram model, so it's
not particularly clever or good.

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:10.645
But you don't need
a P the best model.

00:07:10.645 --> 00:07:12.770
You're just doing data
filtering to create a model.

00:07:12.770 --> 00:07:16.330
So this can be very
crude and fast.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:21.790
So CCNet, which is
a paper out of--

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:27.220
I guess it was a
Facebook back then.

00:07:27.220 --> 00:07:31.960
They-- which we talked
about last time.

00:07:31.960 --> 00:07:38.890
They basically did
this and actually--

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:40.660
OK, that's fine.

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:48.070
So they basically looked
at paragraphs of text.

00:07:48.070 --> 00:07:50.670
And those were the items,
they sorted the paragraphs

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:53.940
by increasing perplexity
and just kept the top 1/3.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:58.500
And this is what they used to
create the first Llama data set.

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:02.730
OK, so this is
heuristic, but if you

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:05.700
had to write down
something really quickly,

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:07.150
this kind of makes sense.

00:08:07.150 --> 00:08:14.400
So Kneser-Ney language modeling
is fast, but it's crude.

00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:16.800
All right.

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.780
I feel like there is some
content that was meant

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:22.990
to go through that's missing.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:26.100
Oh, well, maybe it got deleted.

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:26.800
All right.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:32.200
So you can fit an n-gram
model to your target data,

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:34.650
and then you can use that
to score the raw data

00:08:34.650 --> 00:08:38.700
and choose the top
scoring documents.

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:40.900
Another thing you
can do is fasttext.

00:08:40.900 --> 00:08:44.910
And this is, in some
ways, more popular.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:47.350
This is also from Facebook.

00:08:47.350 --> 00:08:50.950
They released a lot
of open source tools,

00:08:50.950 --> 00:08:57.460
although KenLM, I think,
was created before that.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:01.630
So here, they develop it
for text classification.

00:09:01.630 --> 00:09:05.407
And this paper, I
mean, this is 2016.

00:09:05.407 --> 00:09:07.240
So a lot of people were
developing all sorts

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:08.270
of fancy neural models.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.060
And they said, show that well.

00:09:10.060 --> 00:09:13.790
This actually-- almost linear
classifier worked as well.

00:09:13.790 --> 00:09:15.370
It was much faster.

00:09:15.370 --> 00:09:17.470
So that's why the
software package actually

00:09:17.470 --> 00:09:19.570
became very popular.

00:09:19.570 --> 00:09:24.230
So the motivation here
was that if you're doing,

00:09:24.230 --> 00:09:26.900
let's say, a bag of
words classification.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:31.240
So you have a sentence of
length L, your vocab size is V,

00:09:31.240 --> 00:09:34.850
and you want to classify
into 64 classes.

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:38.710
Then immediately, you
have to define this V

00:09:38.710 --> 00:09:43.310
by K matrix, which if
V and K are both large,

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:48.830
then this gets pretty big, and
you get into sparsity issues,

00:09:48.830 --> 00:09:52.460
and the way it would work is
that you take your documents,

00:09:52.460 --> 00:09:54.990
and then you just do
linear classification.

00:09:54.990 --> 00:09:57.570
So that's fairly
straightforward.

00:09:57.570 --> 00:09:59.940
So the problem is that the
number of parameters is huge.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:05.750
So fasttext says, well,
let me just do a linear--

00:10:05.750 --> 00:10:08.310
basically dimensionality
reduction.

00:10:08.310 --> 00:10:12.860
So instead, I'm going
to map my vocab space

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:17.210
into a hidden dimension,
which is smaller than K,

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:20.600
possibly smaller than
K, 16, and then I'm

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:23.430
going to classify from H.

00:10:23.430 --> 00:10:26.840
So notice that when you
look at the prediction,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:30.090
the forward pass,
there's no non-linearity.

00:10:30.090 --> 00:10:31.380
It's just a linear classifier.

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:34.190
You can also think about it
as a matrix factorization

00:10:34.190 --> 00:10:35.100
if you want.

00:10:35.100 --> 00:10:38.690
And the number of parameters
is greatly reduced.

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:43.650
So the implementation, I
think, is reasonably optimized.

00:10:43.650 --> 00:10:44.430
It's parallelized.

00:10:44.430 --> 00:10:45.995
And they use asynchronous SGD.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:52.060
So here's-- OK.

00:10:52.060 --> 00:10:56.430
So the question is,
this is bag of words.

00:10:56.430 --> 00:10:59.490
And we want to not
just look at words,

00:10:59.490 --> 00:11:01.240
but we want to look at n-grams.

00:11:01.240 --> 00:11:07.780
So this paper also extends to
n-grams in a fairly simple way.

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:10.380
So you take a sentence,
and you get some--

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.500
chop it up into n-grams.

00:11:13.500 --> 00:11:16.470
And here, the problem is
that the number of bigrams

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:18.640
can get quite large.

00:11:18.640 --> 00:11:20.620
And it actually
can be unbounded.

00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:23.350
So remember, when we
talked about tokenizers.

00:11:23.350 --> 00:11:25.920
If you just chop up into
words, you're going to get--

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:27.670
You know how many
words there are.

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:30.820
So the simple thing is
that you just do hashing.

00:11:30.820 --> 00:11:33.790
So you define some number
of bins, maybe 10 million.

00:11:33.790 --> 00:11:35.250
But in this example, eight.

00:11:35.250 --> 00:11:42.190
And then you just hash
every bigram into that.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:48.620
So the cat maps to two and
cat in maps to one, and so on.

00:11:48.620 --> 00:11:53.570
So of course, there
might be some collisions,

00:11:53.570 --> 00:11:59.170
but just live with that.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:03.050
I mean, in some sense,
when you minimize the loss,

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.210
collisions are accounted for.

00:12:05.210 --> 00:12:10.100
If there's two words that have
nothing to do with each other,

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.220
then the weight is going
to somehow represent

00:12:12.220 --> 00:12:14.650
maybe the average of the two.

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:20.920
And also note that often, we use
fast text classification with K

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:24.290
equals 2 classes, is this
document good or is it bad?

00:12:24.290 --> 00:12:27.770
And in that case,
this is just normal.

00:12:27.770 --> 00:12:32.890
Basically binary classification,
linear classification.

00:12:32.890 --> 00:12:36.750
So of course, it doesn't take
too much imagination to imagine.

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:38.500
Well, if you can do
linear classification,

00:12:38.500 --> 00:12:42.890
you can use fancier models,
such as BERT or even Llama.

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:45.630
The only thing is that
it's going to be slower.

00:12:45.630 --> 00:12:47.690
And therefore, you
run into this trade

00:12:47.690 --> 00:12:51.437
off, where if you use a giant
model to do classification,

00:12:51.437 --> 00:12:53.270
maybe you should use
those flops to actually

00:12:53.270 --> 00:12:54.210
just train the model.

00:12:54.210 --> 00:12:56.420
So that's the only concern.

00:12:56.420 --> 00:12:58.710
Because the web is very large.

00:12:58.710 --> 00:13:01.950
And remember, you were filtering
the web down quite a bit.

00:13:01.950 --> 00:13:05.130
So you're going to spend
a lot of compute on,

00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:08.700
let's say, you're filtering
down to 1% of the web.

00:13:08.700 --> 00:13:14.040
So that means you
have your classifier.

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:18.560
That amount of compute you
spend has to be 100th of taking

00:13:18.560 --> 00:13:21.235
a forward pass
through the data set.

00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:27.380
So the final method
I'm going to talk about

00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:32.030
is this thing called data
selection for language models

00:13:32.030 --> 00:13:35.060
using importance sampling.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:37.910
And here, the basic
idea is that you have

00:13:37.910 --> 00:13:39.330
your R. It's a raw data set.

00:13:39.330 --> 00:13:42.760
You have your target data.

00:13:42.760 --> 00:13:45.210
And then I'm going to
build an importance weight

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:51.420
estimator, which is the analog
of the language n-gram model

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:53.050
or the fasttext classifier.

00:13:53.050 --> 00:13:58.590
And then I'm going to use that
to get a bunch of documents.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:02.520
So first, just a kind of a
quick refresher of importance

00:14:02.520 --> 00:14:04.920
re-sampling, I guess.

00:14:04.920 --> 00:14:08.460
So you have-- this shows
up in many contexts,

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:14.370
like particle filtering
and Monte Carlo methods.

00:14:14.370 --> 00:14:17.050
So you have a
target distribution.

00:14:17.050 --> 00:14:19.110
So it's a distribution,
not a data set,

00:14:19.110 --> 00:14:22.150
where you want to ideally
draw samples from this.

00:14:22.150 --> 00:14:24.930
But you only have a
proposal distribution Q,

00:14:24.930 --> 00:14:26.560
where you can draw samples.

00:14:26.560 --> 00:14:31.950
So for example, if you
have a vocab of 0, 1, 2, 3,

00:14:31.950 --> 00:14:34.570
let's say this is your
target distribution.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.970
And your proposal
distribution is this.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:45.710
Now, what do you do
is you sample from Q

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:47.820
because that's the only
thing you can sample from.

00:14:47.820 --> 00:14:50.750
So let's say you get
a bunch of samples.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:56.130
So notice that Q has a
lot of more mass on zero.

00:14:56.130 --> 00:14:58.910
So you get a lot of more zeros.

00:14:58.910 --> 00:15:01.550
And then what you're going
to do is you compute weights

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:08.150
over your samples by looking at
this importance ratio, p over q.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:11.220
So you're trying to make up for
the fact that I sampled from q.

00:15:11.220 --> 00:15:13.590
So I want to divide that
out or I really want p.

00:15:13.590 --> 00:15:20.480
And you normalize and then
get-- you get a distribution

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:22.590
over your samples.

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:27.050
And now, you resample, and you--

00:15:27.050 --> 00:15:30.270
that balances out
the distribution.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:33.980
So now, you see that there's
more 3s because p had more

00:15:33.980 --> 00:15:38.150
probability distribution
on mass on 3.

00:15:38.150 --> 00:15:44.670
So this is a fairly elementary,
but it's a building block.

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:47.530
Now, we go to how we
do data selection.

00:15:47.530 --> 00:15:50.710
So we have a target data
set, not a distribution.

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.020
We have a data set,
which is small.

00:15:53.020 --> 00:15:55.650
And we also have
this raw data set,

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.560
which we're going to call
the proposal data set, Dq,

00:15:58.560 --> 00:15:59.940
which is large.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:04.710
So you could-- let's
just fit a distribution p

00:16:04.710 --> 00:16:08.070
to Dp, theta
distribution q to Dq,

00:16:08.070 --> 00:16:12.210
and do the importance
sampling that I we

00:16:12.210 --> 00:16:16.020
talked about just
two minutes ago.

00:16:16.020 --> 00:16:21.310
Now, the main problem is
that Dp is really small.

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:23.530
Remember, target distribution
is high quality data.

00:16:23.530 --> 00:16:25.960
You don't have that much of
it, which is the whole point.

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:27.580
You're trying to get more of it.

00:16:27.580 --> 00:16:29.050
So you don't have
that much of it.

00:16:29.050 --> 00:16:32.010
And it's too small to
estimate a good model.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:36.820
So again, the idea is you
just use hash n-grams.

00:16:36.820 --> 00:16:38.560
So this is something
that we already

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.210
saw with fasttext as well.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:47.810
You take whatever text you
have, and then you essentially--

00:16:47.810 --> 00:16:52.840
so you basically hash all the--

00:16:52.840 --> 00:16:54.230
we're doing unigrams for now.

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:57.010
So you hash each unigram.

00:16:57.010 --> 00:17:03.730
And you basically
count the number--

00:17:03.730 --> 00:17:08.890
estimate the probability
of every hashed n-gram.

00:17:08.890 --> 00:17:14.119
So in this case, the cat
in the hat hashes to this.

00:17:14.119 --> 00:17:16.119
Notice that there's some
hash collisions though,

00:17:16.119 --> 00:17:18.530
and the hat hatch
into the same value.

00:17:18.530 --> 00:17:19.579
But whatever.

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:20.980
This is all crude.

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:24.910
And then you just estimate the
probability of each of these--

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.040
the probabilities.

00:17:27.040 --> 00:17:27.960
OK.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:33.010
And then to evaluate the
probability of a new text,

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:40.070
you hash the new text, and
you multiply the probabilities

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:40.590
together.

00:17:40.590 --> 00:17:42.020
OK, so I got a
little bit unlucky,

00:17:42.020 --> 00:17:44.880
and the probability
gets zeroed out.

00:17:44.880 --> 00:17:47.240
You can do some
smoothing if you want.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:49.860
So it turns out in Python,
hash is non-deterministic.

00:17:49.860 --> 00:17:54.650
So every time I run this, I'm
going to get something else.

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:59.400
And the paper shows that
this indeed helps a bit.

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:02.100
The games are not super massive.

00:18:02.100 --> 00:18:07.430
But compared to doing fasttext,
which is the heuristic

00:18:07.430 --> 00:18:10.970
classification, the gains
on the glue benchmark using

00:18:10.970 --> 00:18:12.650
BERT style models.

00:18:12.650 --> 00:18:14.870
There is some lift.

00:18:14.870 --> 00:18:18.480
And the comparison
with fasttext.

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:21.110
So the main idea
here is that modeling

00:18:21.110 --> 00:18:24.090
a whole distribution
is, in some sense,

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.230
a more principled approach.

00:18:25.230 --> 00:18:30.590
Because you want to sample
data from your distribution,

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:32.390
and you have a different
distribution q.

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:35.710
And now, you're trying to
essentially compensate.

00:18:35.710 --> 00:18:39.760
And so this could be
better for diversity

00:18:39.760 --> 00:18:41.760
because you're trying to
match the distribution.

00:18:41.760 --> 00:18:45.060
Whereas fasttext,
you're essentially

00:18:45.060 --> 00:18:49.890
trying to classify whether
something is in the distribution

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:50.710
or not.

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:53.130
And it doesn't
have any guarantees

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:56.910
about matching the distribution.

00:18:56.910 --> 00:18:59.770
But it's also fast
like fasttext.

00:18:59.770 --> 00:19:04.930
And both can be improved
by having better models.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:08.050
So instead of using linear
models based on n-grams,

00:19:08.050 --> 00:19:09.100
you can increase n.

00:19:09.100 --> 00:19:15.120
You can use neural
approaches as well.

00:19:15.120 --> 00:19:20.010
OK, so just to
wrap up this part.

00:19:20.010 --> 00:19:23.500
So we looked at n-gram
models, linear classifiers,

00:19:23.500 --> 00:19:25.620
and importance sampling.

00:19:25.620 --> 00:19:27.510
But the general
framework, I think,

00:19:27.510 --> 00:19:32.350
is something that maybe
worth taking away,

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:36.040
which is that the setup
given a target data set

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:38.230
and a raw data set,
find the subset that's

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:40.220
similar to the target.

00:19:40.220 --> 00:19:44.210
And all these methods
have the basic recipe,

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:46.420
which is estimate
some of model based

00:19:46.420 --> 00:19:49.930
on the raw data and the target
data set, which gives you

00:19:49.930 --> 00:19:52.570
a scoring function,
and then keep examples

00:19:52.570 --> 00:19:55.280
in the raw data set based
on that scoring function.

00:19:55.280 --> 00:19:57.440
Usually high scores are kept.

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:02.620
So just to walk through
this in the KenLM case,

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:08.020
the score is simply
the probability of--

00:20:08.020 --> 00:20:10.540
or I guess maybe the
perplexity, if you

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:15.010
want to normalize by the number
of tokens, under the target.

00:20:15.010 --> 00:20:17.210
So r is not used here.

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:22.420
And then you keep examples with
score greater than threshold.

00:20:22.420 --> 00:20:26.260
And you can randomize a little
bit around the threshold

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:28.180
if you want.

00:20:28.180 --> 00:20:31.960
So you can also use a
discriminative classifier,

00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:35.050
where the score is
what's the probability

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:38.410
that this example came
from the target as opposed

00:20:38.410 --> 00:20:40.430
to the raw data set.

00:20:40.430 --> 00:20:43.270
And then you keep examples,
where the score is

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:46.180
of greater than some threshold.

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:48.310
And then importance
sampling says

00:20:48.310 --> 00:20:53.740
the score is the ratio of two
generative models, which could

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:57.400
be n-gram models in this case.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:02.870
p of-- the target distribution
over the raw distribution.

00:21:02.870 --> 00:21:07.180
And to use this score, this is
really the importance weights.

00:21:07.180 --> 00:21:11.380
You resample with probability
proportional to that.

00:21:11.380 --> 00:21:14.170
So at a high level,
all of them are doing

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:16.450
essentially the same thing.

00:21:16.450 --> 00:21:19.060
You have-- you fit some
of distribution that

00:21:19.060 --> 00:21:22.670
tells you what looks like
target as opposed to raw,

00:21:22.670 --> 00:21:25.950
and then you apply that
to the raw data set.

00:21:29.860 --> 00:21:30.970
OK.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:34.340
Maybe I'll pause here in
case there's any questions.

00:21:37.120 --> 00:21:38.590
Yeah.

00:21:38.590 --> 00:21:42.190
It's sort of a high level
philosophical thing.

00:21:42.190 --> 00:21:47.440
We have an idea of what good
data is, but we've kind of not

00:21:47.440 --> 00:21:50.100
talked about what
that's actually like.

00:21:52.990 --> 00:21:55.220
Like a good document looks like.

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:58.810
So what this is doing is like
making sure that the words fit

00:21:58.810 --> 00:22:02.680
next to each other when you
have an environment like that,

00:22:02.680 --> 00:22:04.490
one comes after the other.

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:10.960
But it doesn't ensure that it
makes any sense on like a larger

00:22:10.960 --> 00:22:15.200
level or if you want that just
increase the n and the n-gram?

00:22:15.200 --> 00:22:15.700
Yeah.

00:22:15.700 --> 00:22:17.920
So the question
seems, I think, is

00:22:17.920 --> 00:22:20.900
that if you're using
an n-gram model,

00:22:20.900 --> 00:22:23.210
it's only looking
at local contexts.

00:22:23.210 --> 00:22:26.080
And maybe that's not great
for assessing quality.

00:22:26.080 --> 00:22:28.370
Because if you shuffle
everything around,

00:22:28.370 --> 00:22:30.360
it still looks good
to the Ngram model.

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:34.520
So there's definitely
that danger

00:22:34.520 --> 00:22:37.803
that it's very easy
to adversarially gain.

00:22:37.803 --> 00:22:39.470
If you can construct,
you can definitely

00:22:39.470 --> 00:22:43.440
construct examples that the
n-gram model thinks is high.

00:22:43.440 --> 00:22:47.930
I mean, I showed you one that
does happens to be pretty high.

00:22:47.930 --> 00:22:53.550
But somehow, on average,
you're doing OK.

00:22:53.550 --> 00:22:56.690
And in some sense,
maybe if a document

00:22:56.690 --> 00:22:59.330
looks like grammatical
English, and it

00:22:59.330 --> 00:23:02.840
looks like news or
some scientific paper,

00:23:02.840 --> 00:23:05.780
all you want to make sure
is that that model assigns

00:23:05.780 --> 00:23:07.050
high probability to that.

00:23:07.050 --> 00:23:14.210
If you get some bad examples,
it's not the end of the world.

00:23:14.210 --> 00:23:14.960
Yeah.

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:18.560
So we should think about
these as just filtering out

00:23:18.560 --> 00:23:21.090
true nonsense web
pages that you--

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:21.800
Yeah.

00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:24.110
I would say this
is filtering out

00:23:24.110 --> 00:23:29.520
true nonsense is I think
a good way to look at it.

00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:31.440
As I go through some
examples, I think

00:23:31.440 --> 00:23:36.030
maybe it will become a
bit clearer that this

00:23:36.030 --> 00:23:39.240
is-- these classifiers are
not just used for quality.

00:23:39.240 --> 00:23:41.110
And for some of the
other use cases,

00:23:41.110 --> 00:23:44.985
I think it's much more maybe
compelling why it should work.

00:23:44.985 --> 00:23:45.485
OK.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:51.480
So the same data
filtering machinery

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:53.320
can be used on different tasks.

00:23:53.320 --> 00:23:56.400
So I'm going to talk about
language identification, quality

00:23:56.400 --> 00:23:58.330
filtering, and
toxicity filtering.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:01.650
So language
identification, the idea

00:24:01.650 --> 00:24:03.988
is that you want to find
text of a specific language.

00:24:03.988 --> 00:24:04.780
Maybe it's English.

00:24:04.780 --> 00:24:07.050
Maybe it's Japanese.

00:24:07.050 --> 00:24:10.860
And you could ask,
well-- you could just

00:24:10.860 --> 00:24:12.220
train on all the languages.

00:24:12.220 --> 00:24:14.130
Why don't you do that?

00:24:14.130 --> 00:24:18.210
The problem is that
it can often be

00:24:18.210 --> 00:24:23.520
difficult to do the curation and
processing of high quality data

00:24:23.520 --> 00:24:25.390
in a given language.

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:31.640
And there's also the
problem that if you train--

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:34.400
let's say you only care
about one language.

00:24:34.400 --> 00:24:37.040
Now, if you have data from
all the other languages,

00:24:37.040 --> 00:24:41.740
you're going to be spending less
compute and tokens on any given

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:45.220
languages on the given language.

00:24:45.220 --> 00:24:47.882
So this was-- and that
could hurt your performance

00:24:47.882 --> 00:24:48.590
in that language.

00:24:48.590 --> 00:24:54.460
For example, bloom, which
was trained in about 2022,

00:24:54.460 --> 00:24:59.290
I believe, was only
trained on 30% English.

00:24:59.290 --> 00:25:01.270
And as a result, the
English performance

00:25:01.270 --> 00:25:03.800
maybe was not as good
as it could have been.

00:25:03.800 --> 00:25:05.690
It was strong on
other languages.

00:25:05.690 --> 00:25:09.380
So there, in a compute
limited regime,

00:25:09.380 --> 00:25:14.590
you're definitely at risk
of compromising the quality

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:17.870
of the language you care about.

00:25:17.870 --> 00:25:21.230
Now, that said, if you
have enough capacity

00:25:21.230 --> 00:25:23.620
and you're training
huge models, then you

00:25:23.620 --> 00:25:25.520
can definitely train
multilingual models,

00:25:25.520 --> 00:25:28.560
and there could be even positive
transfer between languages.

00:25:28.560 --> 00:25:30.230
So all the kind
of frontier models

00:25:30.230 --> 00:25:35.360
are trained on heavily,
multilingual, sometimes even

00:25:35.360 --> 00:25:40.010
hundreds of languages,
like 100 plus languages.

00:25:40.010 --> 00:25:45.050
So one simple way to
do this is fasttext

00:25:45.050 --> 00:25:49.440
is a toolkit for training
simple classifiers.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:52.560
It also comes with some
off the shelf models.

00:25:52.560 --> 00:25:54.410
And one of the ones
that typically gets used

00:25:54.410 --> 00:26:00.140
is they have a language
identification model.

00:26:00.140 --> 00:26:03.540
And this is just off
the shelf classifier.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:05.580
It supports a lot of
different languages.

00:26:05.580 --> 00:26:11.570
And it was trained on basically
multilingual sites, including

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:14.330
Wikipedia, which has a lot
of different languages,

00:26:14.330 --> 00:26:18.500
as translation sites, and
for some reason, Southeast

00:26:18.500 --> 00:26:21.140
European news.

00:26:21.140 --> 00:26:24.790
So DOMA uses this.

00:26:24.790 --> 00:26:27.340
They basically run the
fasttext classifier

00:26:27.340 --> 00:26:33.610
and keep pages with English
probability greater than 0.5.

00:26:33.610 --> 00:26:34.640
So let's try it out.

00:26:34.640 --> 00:26:39.550
So there's this model language
ID that you can just download.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:43.030
And then let's see
how well it does.

00:26:43.030 --> 00:26:47.750
So the quick brown fox
jumps over the lazy dog.

00:26:47.750 --> 00:26:49.386
Let's see where are
the predictions.

00:26:56.500 --> 00:26:57.540
OK, I don't know why--

00:26:57.540 --> 00:26:58.040
OK.

00:26:58.040 --> 00:26:58.700
There we go.

00:26:58.700 --> 00:27:02.890
So-- OK.

00:27:02.890 --> 00:27:05.180
So this says English.

00:27:05.180 --> 00:27:06.320
This is the label, English.

00:27:06.320 --> 00:27:09.650
And the probability
of English is 0.71.

00:27:09.650 --> 00:27:12.350
So I mean, it would have
guessed it would be higher.

00:27:12.350 --> 00:27:15.220
It looks pretty English to me.

00:27:15.220 --> 00:27:18.310
If you duplicate the
sentence, the probability

00:27:18.310 --> 00:27:21.490
isn't changing, which is good.

00:27:21.490 --> 00:27:25.520
Saying the same thing
doesn't make it more English.

00:27:25.520 --> 00:27:29.880
There's some informal English,
which this is kind of weird.

00:27:29.880 --> 00:27:34.100
This is, I guess, more English
than the quick brown fox.

00:27:34.100 --> 00:27:35.570
German.

00:27:35.570 --> 00:27:38.210
Actually gets classified fairly.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:39.968
Oh, sorry, this is German.

00:27:39.968 --> 00:27:41.010
Now, the label is German.

00:27:41.010 --> 00:27:41.690
OK, here.

00:27:41.690 --> 00:27:43.370
That is German.

00:27:43.370 --> 00:27:48.050
Math gets classified
pretty weakly as English.

00:27:48.050 --> 00:27:53.960
Code is highest
is, like Russian.

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:58.410
This is-- hello is
apparently Italian.

00:27:58.410 --> 00:27:59.520
This is French.

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:00.530
Bonjour.

00:28:00.530 --> 00:28:02.720
That's good.

00:28:02.720 --> 00:28:04.430
And this is-- I
guess, a tricky one,

00:28:04.430 --> 00:28:07.560
where I put in some Spanish,
and also some English.

00:28:07.560 --> 00:28:10.730
And I guess it favors
the Spanish in this case.

00:28:10.730 --> 00:28:13.020
OK, so this gives you a sense--

00:28:13.020 --> 00:28:15.588
I mean, it's always good to
play with these classifiers.

00:28:15.588 --> 00:28:17.630
Just because it's a
classifier, you can download,

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:21.690
and everyone uses, it doesn't
mean it works all the time.

00:28:21.690 --> 00:28:25.260
So I think obviously,
for short sentences,

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:27.810
it's less reliable because
there's just less information

00:28:27.810 --> 00:28:29.170
about what's happening.

00:28:29.170 --> 00:28:31.620
Low resource languages is tough.

00:28:31.620 --> 00:28:35.430
There's a worry that for
things that don't really--

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:41.220
dialects of English, this
could be viewed as not English.

00:28:41.220 --> 00:28:42.750
I think if you
want to distinguish

00:28:42.750 --> 00:28:46.680
between similar languages, that
also can be easily confused

00:28:46.680 --> 00:28:48.005
and code switching.

00:28:48.005 --> 00:28:49.630
I don't even know
what ground truth is,

00:28:49.630 --> 00:28:55.320
but it'll do, hopefully,
one of the languages.

00:28:55.320 --> 00:28:57.250
So just talking
through one case study,

00:28:57.250 --> 00:29:02.850
open web math was this
paper from two years ago.

00:29:02.850 --> 00:29:05.610
And the goal here is to
curate a large corpus

00:29:05.610 --> 00:29:09.630
of mathematical text, where
here, I'm saying that--

00:29:09.630 --> 00:29:12.930
let's assume that
math is a language.

00:29:12.930 --> 00:29:15.190
So first, they use
some rules to filter.

00:29:15.190 --> 00:29:20.680
Then they train KenLM on this
large data set of proofs,

00:29:20.680 --> 00:29:23.230
called proof file, and
kept it if the perplexity

00:29:23.230 --> 00:29:24.790
was below some threshold.

00:29:24.790 --> 00:29:27.760
And they trained this
fasttext classifier,

00:29:27.760 --> 00:29:30.160
which we have been-- we've
talked about to predict

00:29:30.160 --> 00:29:31.780
mathematical writing.

00:29:31.780 --> 00:29:37.580
This is a hacky, but they set
the threshold of the classifier.

00:29:37.580 --> 00:29:42.290
If it's identified as math based
on rules to be relatively low.

00:29:42.290 --> 00:29:44.960
And if it's not identified
as math according to rules,

00:29:44.960 --> 00:29:48.850
it's needs to be higher.

00:29:48.850 --> 00:29:52.400
And as a result, they
produced 15 billion tokens,

00:29:52.400 --> 00:29:54.520
and they trained some
models that do better

00:29:54.520 --> 00:29:56.980
than models that were trained
on 20 times more data that

00:29:56.980 --> 00:29:58.400
weren't really specified.

00:29:58.400 --> 00:30:04.570
So this is one of a nice
examples of the utility of data

00:30:04.570 --> 00:30:05.270
filtering.

00:30:05.270 --> 00:30:06.950
You could train
on all this data.

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:09.370
But if you care about, let's
say, a particular domain

00:30:09.370 --> 00:30:12.340
like math, you can just go
out, and get more of it,

00:30:12.340 --> 00:30:14.090
and target that data set.

00:30:14.090 --> 00:30:16.986
And if you train on that, you
can be much more data efficient.

00:30:20.210 --> 00:30:22.590
So let's talk about
quality filtering.

00:30:22.590 --> 00:30:24.140
So quality filtering,
obviously, is

00:30:24.140 --> 00:30:27.350
something that doesn't really--

00:30:27.350 --> 00:30:29.880
I mean, it's a catch all
for a bunch of things

00:30:29.880 --> 00:30:32.570
and what is quality.

00:30:32.570 --> 00:30:37.730
So remember that some papers
explicitly don't use model

00:30:37.730 --> 00:30:39.500
based quality filtering.

00:30:39.500 --> 00:30:42.560
But more recent papers
have just kind of given in

00:30:42.560 --> 00:30:48.530
and say, well, I mean, it
just works a lot better.

00:30:48.530 --> 00:30:52.730
So GPT-3, we talked a little bit
about this last time, trained

00:30:52.730 --> 00:30:55.550
a quality classifier by
taking positive samples

00:30:55.550 --> 00:30:57.950
from the high quality
sources that they

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:02.010
had, or rather the non-Common
Crawl sources they had.

00:31:02.010 --> 00:31:04.170
And negatives are
just Common Crawl.

00:31:04.170 --> 00:31:09.530
They train a linear
classifier and keep documents

00:31:09.530 --> 00:31:12.080
stochastically based
on this score, which

00:31:12.080 --> 00:31:15.110
is something that
just turns numbers,

00:31:15.110 --> 00:31:20.320
basically sharpens
the threshold.

00:31:20.320 --> 00:31:26.260
And the first Llma
paper, they used

00:31:26.260 --> 00:31:28.160
pages referenced by Wikipedia.

00:31:28.160 --> 00:31:29.840
So not Wikipedia as positives.

00:31:29.840 --> 00:31:32.110
Negatives are just
sampled from Common Crawl,

00:31:32.110 --> 00:31:35.360
and then they keep documents
that are classified positive.

00:31:35.360 --> 00:31:37.330
I don't think there
was actually sampling

00:31:37.330 --> 00:31:39.730
of that, which is interesting.

00:31:39.730 --> 00:31:43.120
phi-1 is another paper that--

00:31:43.120 --> 00:31:46.540
their philosophy
was interesting.

00:31:46.540 --> 00:31:50.090
They really wanted high quality
data that looked like textbooks,

00:31:50.090 --> 00:31:53.110
and they wanted to train
a very small model.

00:31:53.110 --> 00:31:57.090
So they trained on synthetic
data but also filtered data.

00:31:57.090 --> 00:31:58.840
So for the filtered
data, what they did is

00:31:58.840 --> 00:32:01.610
they took the Python
subset of the stack.

00:32:01.610 --> 00:32:04.210
Remember, the stack is
this pre-processed data

00:32:04.210 --> 00:32:06.740
set coming from GitHub of code.

00:32:06.740 --> 00:32:10.900
And then they define the
prompt, which effectively

00:32:10.900 --> 00:32:15.580
asked GPT-4 to determine
the educational value

00:32:15.580 --> 00:32:18.450
for a student whose goal is to
learn basic coding concepts.

00:32:18.450 --> 00:32:26.070
And then they prompted GPT-4 and
classified 100K documents from

00:32:26.070 --> 00:32:27.460
this Python subset.

00:32:27.460 --> 00:32:29.470
And that became the
positive examples.

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:31.950
They train a random
forest classifier

00:32:31.950 --> 00:32:36.210
on these examples,
where the embeddings fed

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.460
into a random classifier were
from some pre-trained model.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:43.590
And then they
selected data from R

00:32:43.590 --> 00:32:47.410
that is classified
by the classifier.

00:32:47.410 --> 00:32:49.530
So they run the classifier,
then over all of R,

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:51.490
and then get that data.

00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:54.840
So this is interesting because
there is a random forest

00:32:54.840 --> 00:32:55.930
classifier.

00:32:55.930 --> 00:33:00.180
And in this case,
where does T come from?

00:33:00.180 --> 00:33:02.520
Well, T doesn't exist a priori.

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:07.320
T comes from prompting
GPT-4 with some prompt.

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:10.740
So this is something that
you'll see more increasingly

00:33:10.740 --> 00:33:14.880
as the models get better,
is that instead of looking

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:18.340
at sources like, well,
maybe Books1 is great,

00:33:18.340 --> 00:33:22.750
or maybe Wikipedia is great,
now, if you have a good model,

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:27.400
you can just ask the model
for the type of data you want.

00:33:27.400 --> 00:33:29.350
Maybe I want more
chemistry data,

00:33:29.350 --> 00:33:31.740
or I want more
mathematical data that

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:36.040
does a lot of proofs or
elementary math or something,

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:39.870
and you just let
the language model

00:33:39.870 --> 00:33:43.360
a fairly sophisticated
language model give you T.

00:33:43.360 --> 00:33:46.230
And now once you have
T, you turn the recipe

00:33:46.230 --> 00:33:49.710
that we've been
talking about so far.

00:33:49.710 --> 00:33:53.727
So the phi-1 results,
again, looks good.

00:33:53.727 --> 00:33:56.310
So they had a baseline, where
they just took the Python subset

00:33:56.310 --> 00:34:00.480
of the stack, which was this
R. And the performance on human

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:05.400
eval, which is a coding data
set was only 12% after 96K steps

00:34:05.400 --> 00:34:06.190
of training.

00:34:06.190 --> 00:34:08.699
And then they trained the
exact same model architecture

00:34:08.699 --> 00:34:11.230
on this new fancy data set.

00:34:11.230 --> 00:34:15.550
And it was already
at 17 after 36.

00:34:15.550 --> 00:34:18.610
So they declared success.

00:34:18.610 --> 00:34:24.520
So these are some examples
of quality filtering.

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:28.659
Any questions about
quality filtering?

00:34:28.659 --> 00:34:30.489
Yeah.

00:34:30.489 --> 00:34:33.489
We can get away with using
the most expensive model

00:34:33.489 --> 00:34:37.400
in the target data set using the
LLM and not using LLM like you--

00:34:37.400 --> 00:34:37.900
[INAUDIBLE]

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:38.980
Yeah.

00:34:38.980 --> 00:34:42.560
So the point is that you can--

00:34:42.560 --> 00:34:46.070
why can you get away
with using GPT-4 here?

00:34:46.070 --> 00:34:55.389
Because you're using it only
on to create a 100K examples.

00:34:55.389 --> 00:34:59.380
And to create-- and then using
that to distill a classifier,

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:00.950
which is much faster.

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:03.680
And the 100K is much
less than the size of R,

00:35:03.680 --> 00:35:07.295
which is hundreds of-- tens or
hundreds of millions, probably.

00:35:14.180 --> 00:35:19.790
So let's talk a bit
about toxicity filtering.

00:35:19.790 --> 00:35:23.480
So I'm going to talk
about how the DOMA

00:35:23.480 --> 00:35:26.940
paper does toxicity filtering.

00:35:26.940 --> 00:35:31.580
So there's this data set
called Jigsaw Toxic Comments.

00:35:31.580 --> 00:35:34.430
And this came out
of a project, where

00:35:34.430 --> 00:35:37.560
they were trying to help people
have better discussions online.

00:35:37.560 --> 00:35:39.830
So they wanted to
build a classifier that

00:35:39.830 --> 00:35:43.650
could identify when there
was problematic content.

00:35:43.650 --> 00:35:48.020
So they took the data, comments
on the Wikipedia talk pages

00:35:48.020 --> 00:35:51.140
and had annotators
annotate them with

00:35:51.140 --> 00:35:55.970
toxic, severe toxic, obscene
threat, insult, identity hate.

00:35:55.970 --> 00:36:00.240
And so the DOMA folks train
two fasttext classifiers,

00:36:00.240 --> 00:36:06.750
one which is basically
corresponding to hate,

00:36:06.750 --> 00:36:08.675
and the other one is
corresponding to NSFW.

00:36:11.275 --> 00:36:16.800
So here are some
examples of the data set.

00:36:16.800 --> 00:36:20.560
So let's download the
model and try it out.

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:25.320
So the first example,
are you threatening me

00:36:25.320 --> 00:36:26.890
for dispute neutrality?

00:36:26.890 --> 00:36:31.320
This gets classified
as safe for work.

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:34.650
Because I mean, it seems fine.

00:36:34.650 --> 00:36:36.872
And the second one
is flagged as NSFW.

00:36:40.380 --> 00:36:44.760
And these sentences
are both fine.

00:36:44.760 --> 00:36:47.430
So just give you a sense.

00:36:47.430 --> 00:36:49.480
I don't want to show too
many of these examples,

00:36:49.480 --> 00:36:51.040
but you get the idea.

00:36:51.040 --> 00:36:53.435
You can play it out
with your own time.

00:36:56.130 --> 00:37:01.230
So that's all I'll
say about filtering.

00:37:01.230 --> 00:37:04.680
So now, let's talk
about deduplication.

00:37:04.680 --> 00:37:10.770
So there's two types of
duplicates, exact duplicates.

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:14.150
And the thing to realize
is that the web actually

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:17.090
just inherently has a lot
of exact duplicates based

00:37:17.090 --> 00:37:18.720
on mirroring.

00:37:18.720 --> 00:37:23.660
So if you just look
at Project Gutenberg,

00:37:23.660 --> 00:37:27.500
you'll see that the same
exact site gets mirrored

00:37:27.500 --> 00:37:29.040
on a bunch of different URLs.

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.940
So if you're just crawling,
as Common Crawl does,

00:37:31.940 --> 00:37:34.190
it has no way knowing
that these are mirrors,

00:37:34.190 --> 00:37:38.810
and it just get you
the exact same content.

00:37:38.810 --> 00:37:40.680
And then there's
near-duplicates,

00:37:40.680 --> 00:37:45.600
which is basically the same text
that differ by a few tokens.

00:37:45.600 --> 00:37:49.380
And we're going to show you
algorithms that deal with both.

00:37:49.380 --> 00:37:53.130
So near duplicates,
when does this happen?

00:37:53.130 --> 00:37:56.150
Well, you have terms of
service and licenses,

00:37:56.150 --> 00:37:57.870
like the MIT license.

00:37:57.870 --> 00:38:00.840
This thing shows up I don't
know how many times on the web.

00:38:00.840 --> 00:38:02.870
It's just copy and
pasted everywhere.

00:38:02.870 --> 00:38:04.098
Maybe there's even--

00:38:04.098 --> 00:38:04.640
I don't know.

00:38:04.640 --> 00:38:06.848
Maybe people take it and
actually change some things,

00:38:06.848 --> 00:38:10.440
or they messed up, copy, and
pasting, and remove a comma

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:11.640
or something.

00:38:11.640 --> 00:38:13.800
That's all possible.

00:38:13.800 --> 00:38:15.760
And then there's cases--

00:38:15.760 --> 00:38:21.600
if you look at these data
sets, near duplicates are--

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:24.480
it just gives you an idea of
what near duplicates might

00:38:24.480 --> 00:38:25.890
look like.

00:38:25.890 --> 00:38:27.780
Sometimes, you
have this article,

00:38:27.780 --> 00:38:32.550
where someone just seemed to
have paraphrased best actor

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:34.900
in a negative role for
most impactful character,

00:38:34.900 --> 00:38:37.260
but everything else
seems the same.

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:41.950
This is just like
missing a comma here.

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:45.630
Not really sure where
that comma went off to,

00:38:45.630 --> 00:38:51.270
but there could be just
annotator processing errors.

00:38:51.270 --> 00:38:53.350
And then this one's
kind of interesting.

00:38:53.350 --> 00:38:59.430
There's clearly
this very ad text,

00:38:59.430 --> 00:39:05.560
which they just replace Canada
with USA and some other flights.

00:39:05.560 --> 00:39:08.680
So this probably is generated
from some of template system,

00:39:08.680 --> 00:39:11.710
where they slot it in entities.

00:39:11.710 --> 00:39:16.460
So these near-duplicates
are obviously different.

00:39:16.460 --> 00:39:21.530
But obviously, if you
train on tons of--

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:23.425
once you have this
document, this one

00:39:23.425 --> 00:39:24.800
doesn't give you
that much value.

00:39:24.800 --> 00:39:27.700
Let's just put it that way.

00:39:27.700 --> 00:39:31.420
So in extreme case, so if
you look at C4, remember,

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:33.130
C4 was filtered
based on these set

00:39:33.130 --> 00:39:35.080
of rules that
looked for sentences

00:39:35.080 --> 00:39:37.780
that looked like English.

00:39:37.780 --> 00:39:42.410
You'll see this sentence,
which is indeed English,

00:39:42.410 --> 00:39:46.730
that shows up an
astonishing 61,000 times.

00:39:46.730 --> 00:39:49.750
And for whatever
reason, if you actually

00:39:49.750 --> 00:39:55.520
trace down where this comes
from, not the MIT license.

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:56.950
Oh, I have to-- what?

00:39:56.950 --> 00:39:58.450
OK.

00:39:58.450 --> 00:39:59.870
Should I accept these cookies?

00:39:59.870 --> 00:40:01.420
OK, fine.

00:40:01.420 --> 00:40:04.670
This is random
product from Amazon

00:40:04.670 --> 00:40:09.350
that has graffiti mask
drawings, and there's this text.

00:40:09.350 --> 00:40:12.470
And apparently, this
is-- for whatever reason,

00:40:12.470 --> 00:40:16.140
I don't even know how there's
61,000 copies of this,

00:40:16.140 --> 00:40:19.500
but that's what it is.

00:40:19.500 --> 00:40:24.590
And so the idea is that this
text isn't necessarily bad.

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:26.280
It's perfectly good English.

00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:31.550
But you don't want to take
61,000 epochs over this.

00:40:31.550 --> 00:40:33.750
I mean, it just
kind of pointless.

00:40:33.750 --> 00:40:36.640
So deduplication is
complementary to quality

00:40:36.640 --> 00:40:37.140
filtering.

00:40:37.140 --> 00:40:39.960
Quality filtering says
this piece of data,

00:40:39.960 --> 00:40:41.790
I don't want to
ever train on it.

00:40:41.790 --> 00:40:45.870
Deduplication says,
well, this might be fine,

00:40:45.870 --> 00:40:51.080
but I only want a small number
of these rather than 61,000

00:40:51.080 --> 00:40:52.550
copies.

00:40:52.550 --> 00:40:57.260
So there's been work
showing that deduplication

00:40:57.260 --> 00:40:59.600
makes language models better.

00:40:59.600 --> 00:41:01.400
The first, most obvious
thing is that you

00:41:01.400 --> 00:41:03.120
can train more efficiently.

00:41:03.120 --> 00:41:05.290
Deduplication reduces
the number of tokens.

00:41:05.290 --> 00:41:07.510
And if you haven't
thrown away information,

00:41:07.510 --> 00:41:12.160
then you can save
a lot of effort.

00:41:12.160 --> 00:41:16.655
And then the second is that you
can also avoid memorization.

00:41:16.655 --> 00:41:18.280
So we haven't really
talked about this,

00:41:18.280 --> 00:41:20.910
but language models trained
on data, can memorize that

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:25.200
data, which is bad for
copyright or privacy reasons

00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:28.030
because it can just
regurgitate the training set.

00:41:28.030 --> 00:41:30.730
And deduplication is one tool.

00:41:30.730 --> 00:41:33.690
It's not definitely not
perfect, but it can help

00:41:33.690 --> 00:41:36.103
mitigate some of these risks.

00:41:39.810 --> 00:41:43.380
OK, so here's how to
think about deduplication

00:41:43.380 --> 00:41:44.920
in terms of design space.

00:41:44.920 --> 00:41:48.360
So first, you have to think
about what is the unit

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:49.540
that you're deduplicating.

00:41:49.540 --> 00:41:50.350
What is an item?

00:41:50.350 --> 00:41:51.100
Is it a sentence?

00:41:51.100 --> 00:41:51.850
Is it a paragraph?

00:41:51.850 --> 00:41:53.130
Is it a document?

00:41:53.130 --> 00:41:56.490
The second question
is, how do you match?

00:41:56.490 --> 00:41:57.880
What is considered a duplicate?

00:41:57.880 --> 00:41:59.080
Is it an exact match?

00:41:59.080 --> 00:42:03.940
Is it a fraction
of common subitems?

00:42:03.940 --> 00:42:08.870
And then the final thing
is, what action do you take?

00:42:08.870 --> 00:42:15.010
Do you remove all the instances,
or do you remove all but one?

00:42:15.010 --> 00:42:17.140
OK.

00:42:17.140 --> 00:42:20.590
So the key challenge, and this
is a algorithmic challenge

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:24.040
is that fundamentally,
deduplication is about comparing

00:42:24.040 --> 00:42:26.230
items to other items.

00:42:26.230 --> 00:42:28.588
Quality classification,
you can take an item,

00:42:28.588 --> 00:42:30.380
you can classify it as
high quality or not.

00:42:30.380 --> 00:42:33.260
Deduplication is fundamentally
a pairwise thing.

00:42:33.260 --> 00:42:36.140
And remember, these
data sets are large.

00:42:36.140 --> 00:42:38.570
So you can't-- if you're trying
to do something quadratic,

00:42:38.570 --> 00:42:40.340
that's kind of a no go.

00:42:40.340 --> 00:42:44.440
So you need some of linear
time algorithm to scale,

00:42:44.440 --> 00:42:48.580
but still do something
that's pairwise.

00:42:48.580 --> 00:42:53.800
So the building block of all
of this is hash functions.

00:42:53.800 --> 00:42:56.110
So hash functions,
just as a review,

00:42:56.110 --> 00:42:59.510
is a function that maps an
item, which could be a sentence,

00:42:59.510 --> 00:43:02.020
it could be a document,
to a hash value, which

00:43:02.020 --> 00:43:03.890
is an integer or string.

00:43:03.890 --> 00:43:06.620
And the value is much
smaller than the item.

00:43:06.620 --> 00:43:09.850
And the thing that's
relevant about hash functions

00:43:09.850 --> 00:43:13.510
is that you could potentially
get hash collisions, which

00:43:13.510 --> 00:43:16.390
means that you have two distinct
items that map to the same hash

00:43:16.390 --> 00:43:17.180
value.

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:18.790
So in general, if you've--

00:43:18.790 --> 00:43:19.780
I'm sure you've all--

00:43:19.780 --> 00:43:22.460
I guess all of you have used
dictionaries or hash tables.

00:43:22.460 --> 00:43:24.530
Hash collisions
are generally bad.

00:43:24.530 --> 00:43:26.800
But later, we'll see
that there actually can

00:43:26.800 --> 00:43:30.760
be good in some limited doses.

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:34.240
And there's many types of hash
functions, which trade off

00:43:34.240 --> 00:43:36.730
between efficiency and
collision resistance, which

00:43:36.730 --> 00:43:38.750
means don't collide.

00:43:38.750 --> 00:43:40.570
There's cryptographic
hash functions,

00:43:40.570 --> 00:43:44.290
which are used for security
sensitive applications,

00:43:44.290 --> 00:43:45.470
like in Bitcoin.

00:43:45.470 --> 00:43:46.900
You really don't
want collisions.

00:43:46.900 --> 00:43:50.780
Because otherwise, that means
someone might steal your money,

00:43:50.780 --> 00:43:53.570
or it could compromise things.

00:43:53.570 --> 00:43:56.470
And then there's things where--

00:43:56.470 --> 00:43:58.660
but these are slow.

00:43:58.660 --> 00:44:01.030
But then there's hash
functions, which are generally

00:44:01.030 --> 00:44:04.210
used for hash
tables that are not

00:44:04.210 --> 00:44:06.980
collision resistant but fast.

00:44:06.980 --> 00:44:10.900
And so we're generally going
to go with the latter category

00:44:10.900 --> 00:44:12.890
because we want
things to be fast,

00:44:12.890 --> 00:44:16.810
and we're not doing
cryptography here.

00:44:16.810 --> 00:44:19.930
So we're going to use this thing
called MurmurHash, which takes

00:44:19.930 --> 00:44:22.730
strings and returns values.

00:44:22.730 --> 00:44:24.990
But you can use a bunch
of other hashes as well.

00:44:27.970 --> 00:44:31.400
So let's start with
exact deduplication.

00:44:31.400 --> 00:44:33.350
So here's a simple example.

00:44:33.350 --> 00:44:36.350
The item is just a string,
and I want exact match,

00:44:36.350 --> 00:44:38.750
and I want to
remove all but one.

00:44:38.750 --> 00:44:41.770
So here's a bunch of items.

00:44:41.770 --> 00:44:47.950
And what I'm going to first
do is compute a mapping

00:44:47.950 --> 00:44:54.520
from hash value to the list
of items with that hash.

00:44:54.520 --> 00:45:00.060
And then once I have that, I
keep one item from each group.

00:45:00.060 --> 00:45:03.140
So each group corresponds
to a hash value,

00:45:03.140 --> 00:45:08.600
and in the case of
exact match, assuming

00:45:08.600 --> 00:45:11.300
there's no hash collisions,
then you basically

00:45:11.300 --> 00:45:13.350
do an exact dedupe.

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:16.740
So hello shows up twice here,
and now it shows up once.

00:45:16.740 --> 00:45:20.120
And of course, hello,
uppercase exclamation point

00:45:20.120 --> 00:45:24.620
is just a completely distinct
item because we're doing exact.

00:45:24.620 --> 00:45:29.370
So this is-- the nice thing
is that this is very simple.

00:45:29.370 --> 00:45:30.930
It's clear what you're doing.

00:45:30.930 --> 00:45:32.100
It's high precision.

00:45:32.100 --> 00:45:36.860
So you're never going to throw
away anything that you didn't--

00:45:36.860 --> 00:45:39.950
that you didn't
need or you needed.

00:45:39.950 --> 00:45:41.970
But the con is that you--

00:45:41.970 --> 00:45:45.260
it doesn't work for
near-duplicates.

00:45:45.260 --> 00:45:47.853
And the other thing is
that's nice is that this

00:45:47.853 --> 00:45:49.020
is very simple to implement.

00:45:49.020 --> 00:45:51.150
We kind of wrote this
in a MapReduce way,

00:45:51.150 --> 00:45:54.800
and you can scale it and
parallelize it fairly easily.

00:45:54.800 --> 00:45:57.960
And this is something
that C4 uses, actually,

00:45:57.960 --> 00:45:59.560
to prepare their data set.

00:45:59.560 --> 00:46:02.340
So their unit is
of deduplication

00:46:02.340 --> 00:46:03.970
is three sentence spans.

00:46:03.970 --> 00:46:08.400
They use exact match, and
they remove all but one.

00:46:08.400 --> 00:46:13.420
So one thing that is
always kind of bothered me,

00:46:13.420 --> 00:46:15.510
but I know no one
else seems to care,

00:46:15.510 --> 00:46:20.640
is that when you're looking
at three sentence spans.

00:46:20.640 --> 00:46:22.740
So you have this
document, and then you

00:46:22.740 --> 00:46:25.170
have this three-sentence span.

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:27.060
And if it's marked
as a duplicate,

00:46:27.060 --> 00:46:32.920
you're just going to do surgery
and take those sentences out.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:36.430
And so the resulting document
might not even be coherent.

00:46:36.430 --> 00:46:42.010
But then people say,
who cares, and move on.

00:46:42.010 --> 00:46:47.610
So there's another way
to do deduplication

00:46:47.610 --> 00:46:51.190
that is less MapReduce but
more kind of hash table.

00:46:51.190 --> 00:46:53.910
I think the bloom
filters are-- some of you

00:46:53.910 --> 00:46:56.680
might have seen but just to
go through it because I think

00:46:56.680 --> 00:47:00.490
it's kind of a cool method.

00:47:00.490 --> 00:47:06.890
So this is efficient,
and it's approximate.

00:47:06.890 --> 00:47:08.510
So it's more efficient.

00:47:08.510 --> 00:47:13.490
But obviously, it's not
always doing the exact thing.

00:47:13.490 --> 00:47:17.590
But we're very have not--

00:47:17.590 --> 00:47:21.610
we are not being too picky here.

00:47:21.610 --> 00:47:25.450
So it's the nice thing is
it's a very memory efficient.

00:47:25.450 --> 00:47:28.300
You can update it,
but you can't delete.

00:47:28.300 --> 00:47:31.360
And it's basically a
representation set,

00:47:31.360 --> 00:47:33.160
where if you ask
for set membership,

00:47:33.160 --> 00:47:35.300
and it returns no, then
it's definitely a no.

00:47:35.300 --> 00:47:39.280
But if it returns
yes, then most likely,

00:47:39.280 --> 00:47:41.690
if you set your hyperparameters
right, it's yes.

00:47:41.690 --> 00:47:47.300
But there's a small probability
that you have of no,

00:47:47.300 --> 00:47:49.630
you have a false positive.

00:47:49.630 --> 00:47:53.800
And then you can
set your parameters

00:47:53.800 --> 00:47:56.600
to drive down the false
positive rate if you like.

00:47:56.600 --> 00:48:01.260
OK, so just to walk through
what this looks like.

00:48:01.260 --> 00:48:05.280
So suppose you have five
items, the cat in the hat.

00:48:05.280 --> 00:48:08.900
And later, we'll test
out with some items

00:48:08.900 --> 00:48:12.892
that don't show up in the set.

00:48:12.892 --> 00:48:14.600
The first thing is
you're going to define

00:48:14.600 --> 00:48:20.030
a hash function that maps to m
bins. m is going to be 8 here.

00:48:20.030 --> 00:48:23.840
So to build the
bloom filter, we're

00:48:23.840 --> 00:48:26.790
going to do something
fairly simple.

00:48:26.790 --> 00:48:33.770
You just make your bit
array with a number of bins,

00:48:33.770 --> 00:48:37.650
and then go through every
item, hash it, the hashes to 2.

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:41.520
So I update the second
item, hash it to 7,

00:48:41.520 --> 00:48:45.420
update the seventh
item in the hat.

00:48:45.420 --> 00:48:49.460
So it just populate
this bit array.

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:51.600
And the bit array, I don't
keep track of the items.

00:48:51.600 --> 00:48:55.930
So I have no idea if I have
a false positive or not.

00:48:55.930 --> 00:49:01.750
So let's just sanity check
that every item that I put in

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:03.290
is actually in the item.

00:49:03.290 --> 00:49:08.500
So to query it, you just
compute whether that item

00:49:08.500 --> 00:49:12.400
hashed is in the table.

00:49:12.400 --> 00:49:16.330
And indeed, everything
should pass.

00:49:16.330 --> 00:49:25.250
And now, let's look at the non
items and see how they fare.

00:49:25.250 --> 00:49:29.470
And you'll see that some
of the non-items, you

00:49:29.470 --> 00:49:32.230
get a 0, which is what you want.

00:49:32.230 --> 00:49:34.270
But some of the items,
the bloom filter

00:49:34.270 --> 00:49:36.980
says, oh, actually,
it's in the set.

00:49:36.980 --> 00:49:41.300
So the number of
mistakes here is 4.

00:49:41.300 --> 00:49:43.570
And if you compute the
false positive rate, which

00:49:43.570 --> 00:49:47.920
is the number of mistakes
over the total number of times

00:49:47.920 --> 00:49:52.680
the procedure
produced a positive,

00:49:52.680 --> 00:49:56.300
then the false positive
rate is, in this case,

00:49:56.300 --> 00:49:59.030
0.44, which is pretty bad.

00:49:59.030 --> 00:50:01.040
Now, of course, this is a bin 8.

00:50:01.040 --> 00:50:05.460
And if the bin were to grow,
then this would get better.

00:50:05.460 --> 00:50:06.630
In fact, it grows.

00:50:06.630 --> 00:50:08.860
The error probability is
1 over the number of bins.

00:50:11.432 --> 00:50:14.850
But you look at this and say,
well, that's not really good.

00:50:14.850 --> 00:50:19.760
Because if I want the
error probability to be 10

00:50:19.760 --> 00:50:24.660
to the minus 10, then
I need a lot of bins.

00:50:24.660 --> 00:50:26.880
And that's not going
to hurt on memory.

00:50:26.880 --> 00:50:29.510
So there's a more clever
solution here, which

00:50:29.510 --> 00:50:33.440
is to use more hash functions.

00:50:33.440 --> 00:50:36.330
So let's say I use
two hash functions.

00:50:36.330 --> 00:50:38.720
And I'm going to--

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:43.530
essentially, I still
have the same bit array.

00:50:43.530 --> 00:50:49.320
But for every item now, I'm
going to use hash it twice.

00:50:49.320 --> 00:50:53.590
So the under seed
0 gets hashed to 2.

00:50:53.590 --> 00:50:54.940
I'm going to pop it in.

00:50:54.940 --> 00:50:59.050
And then with seed 1
is going to hash to 5.

00:50:59.050 --> 00:51:00.910
So I'm going to pop it in there.

00:51:00.910 --> 00:51:04.710
So every item gets hashed
into K. Not necessarily

00:51:04.710 --> 00:51:07.210
distinct, but hey, k slots.

00:51:07.210 --> 00:51:11.870
So then I go through the cat,
and then I fill up this table.

00:51:14.730 --> 00:51:21.600
So now, when I do the
querying, I take an element,

00:51:21.600 --> 00:51:25.860
and I'm going to return one
if the table set was set

00:51:25.860 --> 00:51:30.340
to 1 for all K hash functions.

00:51:30.340 --> 00:51:33.040
Because if I put it in,
I have to set K bits.

00:51:33.040 --> 00:51:36.360
And so if I'm testing, I
need to check the K locations

00:51:36.360 --> 00:51:38.810
and check that
they were all set.

00:51:41.940 --> 00:51:44.470
So indeed, all of those paths.

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:46.412
And now, let's look at the--

00:51:50.230 --> 00:51:55.690
so the number of
mistakes now is 3.

00:51:55.690 --> 00:51:58.390
And the false positive
rate decreased.

00:51:58.390 --> 00:52:00.880
So that's great.

00:52:00.880 --> 00:52:02.870
So of course, this
is a toy example,

00:52:02.870 --> 00:52:06.610
but you can really drive
down the false positive rate

00:52:06.610 --> 00:52:10.194
with modest memory spend.

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:16.030
OK, maybe I'll
pause there before I

00:52:16.030 --> 00:52:19.755
go to analyzing this
a bit more formally.

00:52:28.000 --> 00:52:34.000
So let's think about what
happens more generally.

00:52:34.000 --> 00:52:39.590
So let's say we have 1,000
bins, we have 10 cash functions,

00:52:39.590 --> 00:52:41.690
and we're going
to hash 100 items.

00:52:41.690 --> 00:52:45.490
The question is, what is
the false positive rate?

00:52:45.490 --> 00:52:47.360
And the way to
think about this is

00:52:47.360 --> 00:52:49.700
that I'm going to consider
a test input that's

00:52:49.700 --> 00:52:55.340
not in the set, that is going to
hash into a particular location,

00:52:55.340 --> 00:52:56.400
like I.

00:52:56.400 --> 00:52:59.360
And then I'm going
to-- so I'm going

00:52:59.360 --> 00:53:03.800
to now consider putting items,
the n items into the bloom

00:53:03.800 --> 00:53:06.320
filter, and see what is
the probability that it

00:53:06.320 --> 00:53:14.270
hits I. If anything hits
I, then that is a bad sign.

00:53:14.270 --> 00:53:16.590
So let's warm up here.

00:53:16.590 --> 00:53:19.470
So let's say I'm just-- n is 1.

00:53:19.470 --> 00:53:21.200
I'm just inserting one element.

00:53:21.200 --> 00:53:23.160
And K is also 1.

00:53:23.160 --> 00:53:25.200
So I'm only using
one hash function.

00:53:25.200 --> 00:53:27.710
So now, the question
is, what is the chance

00:53:27.710 --> 00:53:31.190
that there is a hash
collision between I

00:53:31.190 --> 00:53:33.470
and whatever element
I'm putting in?

00:53:33.470 --> 00:53:36.660
And the answer to that is just
1 over the number of bins.

00:53:36.660 --> 00:53:39.246
Assuming everything's
kind of independent.

00:53:41.930 --> 00:53:47.800
That's going to be 0.001.

00:53:47.800 --> 00:53:52.060
OK, so now, I'm
still-- n is still 1,

00:53:52.060 --> 00:53:55.210
but I'm going to use
k hash functions.

00:53:55.210 --> 00:54:03.640
So then essentially,
if I'm going to--

00:54:03.640 --> 00:54:07.910
then the criteria is I have
to miss not just one time

00:54:07.910 --> 00:54:11.310
but K times.

00:54:11.310 --> 00:54:11.810
OK.

00:54:11.810 --> 00:54:14.570
So this is the
probability that I miss 1.

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:17.950
So 1 minus that is the
probability that I hit.

00:54:17.950 --> 00:54:23.260
And then that raises to K
times is the probability

00:54:23.260 --> 00:54:30.280
that I have hit on
K, and then y minus

00:54:30.280 --> 00:54:37.840
that is the probability
that I have to miss K times.

00:54:37.840 --> 00:54:43.455
So now, the probability
here goes down a little bit.

00:54:46.190 --> 00:54:51.690
So now, if I'm
inserting n items,

00:54:51.690 --> 00:54:55.160
so I'm asking the
probability that the test

00:54:55.160 --> 00:55:00.030
bin is one after n.

00:55:00.030 --> 00:55:01.850
So now, instead of
missing k times,

00:55:01.850 --> 00:55:05.490
I have to miss K times n times.

00:55:05.490 --> 00:55:10.940
So then basically, this
expression but just K times n.

00:55:10.940 --> 00:55:20.240
And finally, because the test
item is something that I am,

00:55:20.240 --> 00:55:26.220
you're also hashing, so
I get K chances to miss.

00:55:26.220 --> 00:55:28.310
So the false positive
rate actually--

00:55:28.310 --> 00:55:29.900
this is what kind of helps--

00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:34.460
the K really helps is
that I can drive down

00:55:34.460 --> 00:55:37.380
the false positive rate with K.

00:55:37.380 --> 00:55:39.560
So that becomes--

00:55:39.560 --> 00:55:47.640
F goes from 0.63 to 0.01.

00:55:47.640 --> 00:55:52.380
You can actually look
at the expression F

00:55:52.380 --> 00:55:59.820
here and compute the optimal
value of K given a fixed ratio

00:55:59.820 --> 00:56:02.680
as this is an
asymptotic calculation.

00:56:02.680 --> 00:56:08.520
And then you find
that this results in K

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:11.140
is scaling as order M over N.

00:56:11.140 --> 00:56:13.620
So the number of hash
functions you should use

00:56:13.620 --> 00:56:15.990
is on the order of--

00:56:15.990 --> 00:56:21.010
number of bins over the number
of items you're hashing.

00:56:21.010 --> 00:56:27.510
And if you do that,
then f turns out to be,

00:56:27.510 --> 00:56:33.210
0.5, and the false positive
rate is 0.5 to the K.

00:56:33.210 --> 00:56:36.250
So you see that in
this particular case,

00:56:36.250 --> 00:56:40.620
I wasn't optimal because the
optimal value is actually

00:56:40.620 --> 00:56:43.330
K equals 6, and the
false positive rate

00:56:43.330 --> 00:56:49.210
is 0.08 as opposed to 0.0--

00:56:49.210 --> 00:56:54.640
sorry, 0.008 rather than 0.01.

00:56:54.640 --> 00:56:56.510
So you can read more about this.

00:56:56.510 --> 00:56:58.450
There's some lecture
notes that allow

00:56:58.450 --> 00:57:02.260
you to trade off the
compute the memory

00:57:02.260 --> 00:57:06.070
and the false positive rate.

00:57:06.070 --> 00:57:08.860
So the bloom filter has
some hyperparameters

00:57:08.860 --> 00:57:15.790
that allow you to control
whether what your desired

00:57:15.790 --> 00:57:18.950
false positive rate is,
how much memory you have,

00:57:18.950 --> 00:57:22.270
how much compute you're
willing to put in, and so on.

00:57:22.270 --> 00:57:23.650
Yeah.

00:57:23.650 --> 00:57:25.180
I noticed the k went down.

00:57:25.180 --> 00:57:30.325
When it went down, K is
higher than it should be.

00:57:30.325 --> 00:57:37.030
So you're saying that
the optimal K went down.

00:57:37.030 --> 00:57:42.050
And so why does the F go down?

00:57:42.050 --> 00:57:48.210
So the-- let's see.

00:57:48.210 --> 00:57:55.110
So if the K goes down, I
think it's not monotonic.

00:57:55.110 --> 00:57:58.310
So if you use a very--

00:57:58.310 --> 00:58:01.100
I think if you use a very
large value of K, that's

00:58:01.100 --> 00:58:03.710
actually pretty bad
because then you just

00:58:03.710 --> 00:58:06.710
fill up the bloom filter.

00:58:06.710 --> 00:58:10.380
And if you use too small, that's
also not very good either.

00:58:10.380 --> 00:58:12.870
So I think there's a optimum.

00:58:12.870 --> 00:58:16.030
And whether it goes down or up
depends on which side you're on.

00:58:20.360 --> 00:58:26.030
So in Dolma, they set the
false positive rate to 10

00:58:26.030 --> 00:58:29.030
to the negative 15, and they
use a bloom filter to do

00:58:29.030 --> 00:58:30.746
their exact deduplication.

00:58:34.010 --> 00:58:38.400
And they did it at
the paragraph level.

00:58:38.400 --> 00:58:39.340
OK.

00:58:39.340 --> 00:58:42.540
So that was exact deduplication.

00:58:42.540 --> 00:58:45.120
And the problem with
exact deduplication

00:58:45.120 --> 00:58:48.900
is that it doesn't capture some
of these near misses, where

00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:53.620
morally, it's a duplicate, but
we are not able to detect it.

00:58:53.620 --> 00:58:58.950
So now, how do you do
approximate set membership?

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:02.190
And to talk about
approximate set membership,

00:59:02.190 --> 00:59:04.900
you need a notion of
a similarity measure.

00:59:04.900 --> 00:59:08.440
So one common one that's
used is Jaccard similarity.

00:59:08.440 --> 00:59:10.650
So the Jaccard of
two sets, A and B,

00:59:10.650 --> 00:59:14.290
is the ratio of their
intersection over their union.

00:59:14.290 --> 00:59:19.440
So as an example, if
I have 1234 and 1235,

00:59:19.440 --> 00:59:25.210
if you compute the Jaccard,
the intersection has size 3,

00:59:25.210 --> 00:59:32.100
the union has size 5, and
therefore the Jaccard is 0.6.

00:59:32.100 --> 00:59:34.380
So we're going to say
that two documents are

00:59:34.380 --> 00:59:38.090
near duplicates if their
Jaccard similarity exceeds

00:59:38.090 --> 00:59:39.360
some threshold.

00:59:39.360 --> 00:59:42.120
And generally, the threshold
will be fairly high,

00:59:42.120 --> 00:59:47.630
like 0.99 if you
want to only match--

00:59:47.630 --> 00:59:49.740
if you're missing a
comma or something.

00:59:49.740 --> 00:59:52.140
Now, notice that there's
nothing semantic about this.

00:59:52.140 --> 00:59:55.777
You could be missing
a word like not.

00:59:55.777 --> 00:59:57.860
And of course, that changes
the meaning completely

00:59:57.860 --> 00:59:59.190
or some content word.

00:59:59.190 --> 01:00:02.780
But this is just trying
to get documents that look

01:00:02.780 --> 01:00:06.680
fairly superficially similar.

01:00:06.680 --> 01:00:09.030
Now, the algorithmic
challenge is,

01:00:09.030 --> 01:00:11.780
how do you find near
duplicates in linear time?

01:00:11.780 --> 01:00:14.330
So to work up to
that, we're going

01:00:14.330 --> 01:00:18.350
to try to rewrite the
Jaccard similarity, which,

01:00:18.350 --> 01:00:19.610
again, is a pairwise.

01:00:19.610 --> 01:00:21.450
You can't compute
Jaccard of one element.

01:00:21.450 --> 01:00:23.880
You can only compute it
if you have two elements.

01:00:23.880 --> 01:00:26.330
And I'm going to try
to write it in terms

01:00:26.330 --> 01:00:28.610
of a hash function,
which you can compute

01:00:28.610 --> 01:00:31.610
in terms of one element.

01:00:31.610 --> 01:00:35.600
So there is a hash
function called minhash,

01:00:35.600 --> 01:00:39.030
which has the nice property
that the probability

01:00:39.030 --> 01:00:43.500
of a hash collision
is exactly Jaccard AB.

01:00:43.500 --> 01:00:46.560
So normally, remember, you
think of hash collisions

01:00:46.560 --> 01:00:49.750
as to be avoided at all costs.

01:00:49.750 --> 01:00:51.460
You really don't like them.

01:00:51.460 --> 01:00:55.300
But here, it's not that you
want more hash collisions,

01:00:55.300 --> 01:00:58.200
it's just that hash
collisions are something

01:00:58.200 --> 01:01:00.970
that you want to control for.

01:01:00.970 --> 01:01:06.360
You want just the right level
of hash collisions governed

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:07.660
by the similarity.

01:01:07.660 --> 01:01:10.090
So the problem, again,
just to state it properly,

01:01:10.090 --> 01:01:15.340
the similarity is equal to
the probability of collision.

01:01:15.340 --> 01:01:17.642
So the more similar
two things are,

01:01:17.642 --> 01:01:18.850
the more they should collide.

01:01:18.850 --> 01:01:22.920
So that's the intuition.

01:01:22.920 --> 01:01:28.300
So MinHash is
defined as follows.

01:01:28.300 --> 01:01:35.140
You take a set, and you hash
all these elements, remember,

01:01:35.140 --> 01:01:38.650
these return numbers, and you
just take the minimum element.

01:01:38.650 --> 01:01:42.200
So it might-- at first glance,
if you haven't seen this,

01:01:42.200 --> 01:01:45.940
it's not obvious why
just taking the Min

01:01:45.940 --> 01:01:49.090
actually gives you this
property of expectation

01:01:49.090 --> 01:01:51.550
equal to the Jaccard.

01:01:51.550 --> 01:01:54.530
But the argument is
actually fairly simple.

01:01:54.530 --> 01:01:59.860
So the way to think about it is
that you have your five items,

01:01:59.860 --> 01:02:01.520
and you have two sets.

01:02:01.520 --> 01:02:07.510
And I'm going to look at this of
character matrix representation.

01:02:07.510 --> 01:02:09.830
So A has 1234.

01:02:09.830 --> 01:02:12.730
B has 1235.

01:02:12.730 --> 01:02:18.220
And now, the random
hash function

01:02:18.220 --> 01:02:21.680
induces a permutation
over the items.

01:02:21.680 --> 01:02:26.140
So permutation might
be 32145, for example,

01:02:26.140 --> 01:02:28.570
this hash function right there.

01:02:28.570 --> 01:02:33.193
And now, let's look at
which item is first in A,

01:02:33.193 --> 01:02:38.810
and which item is
first in B. OK.

01:02:38.810 --> 01:02:42.590
So that corresponds to
the item corresponding

01:02:42.590 --> 01:02:44.480
to the minimum hash.

01:02:44.480 --> 01:02:54.350
And the item-- each item has the
same probability as being first

01:02:54.350 --> 01:02:57.410
because it's the hash
function you're assuming has--

01:02:57.410 --> 01:03:00.080
it's random.

01:03:00.080 --> 01:03:07.100
So if 1, 2, or 3 are
first, then the MinHash

01:03:07.100 --> 01:03:09.140
is actually going
to be the same.

01:03:09.140 --> 01:03:14.060
Because the minimum--
if these are first,

01:03:14.060 --> 01:03:19.550
the minimum here is equal
to the minimum here.

01:03:19.550 --> 01:03:22.820
But if the minimum--

01:03:22.820 --> 01:03:27.150
if the first-- the minimum
hash is four or five,

01:03:27.150 --> 01:03:31.010
then the first in A will
not be the first in B,

01:03:31.010 --> 01:03:33.780
and the MinHash
will be different.

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:37.230
And then now, you can say,
see that the probability

01:03:37.230 --> 01:03:40.410
of the MinHash being
equal is exactly

01:03:40.410 --> 01:03:46.110
1, 2, or 3, which is identically
exactly the intersection.

01:03:46.110 --> 01:03:49.950
So if you're in the intersection
and that item is first,

01:03:49.950 --> 01:03:53.830
then you're going
to get a collision.

01:03:53.830 --> 01:03:56.730
And if you're not
in the intersection,

01:03:56.730 --> 01:04:00.270
then that one being first,
you will not get a collision

01:04:00.270 --> 01:04:02.932
because you're going to get
the Min for one of them,

01:04:02.932 --> 01:04:05.390
and you're going to get some
other value for the other one.

01:04:10.440 --> 01:04:12.030
OK.

01:04:12.030 --> 01:04:12.700
All right.

01:04:12.700 --> 01:04:16.110
So you can check
this empirically

01:04:16.110 --> 01:04:17.970
if you don't believe me.

01:04:17.970 --> 01:04:21.150
So generate 1,000--

01:04:21.150 --> 01:04:24.030
I guess we're running
this on A and B

01:04:24.030 --> 01:04:26.700
and checking what's the
probability that you

01:04:26.700 --> 01:04:27.910
get a collision.

01:04:27.910 --> 01:04:31.550
And it turns out that
estimated Jaccard card is 0.6.

01:04:31.550 --> 01:04:32.810
It's not exactly 0.6.

01:04:32.810 --> 01:04:37.210
I think there's some rounding,
but it's pretty close.

01:04:37.210 --> 01:04:39.590
So now, we've taken
the Jaccard similarity,

01:04:39.590 --> 01:04:41.950
which is pairwise
function, and reduced it

01:04:41.950 --> 01:04:50.110
to some probabilistic unary
function just on an item.

01:04:50.110 --> 01:04:52.510
But this is actually--
we're far from done.

01:04:52.510 --> 01:04:54.650
Because now, we
can hash our items.

01:04:54.650 --> 01:04:59.740
But a collision doesn't tell
us that these two are similar.

01:04:59.740 --> 01:05:02.560
It's just more likely that
more similar things will

01:05:02.560 --> 01:05:07.000
be hashed together, but we want
something a bit stronger here.

01:05:07.000 --> 01:05:11.800
So this is where locality
sensitive hashing comes.

01:05:11.800 --> 01:05:14.650
And so right now,
so far, we just

01:05:14.650 --> 01:05:21.260
have a hash function that
takes two hashes sets,

01:05:21.260 --> 01:05:26.860
and the sets will collide
if with probability Jaccard.

01:05:26.860 --> 01:05:32.200
But we really want A and B to
collide if and only if, ideally,

01:05:32.200 --> 01:05:37.110
if the Jaccard A and B is
exceeding some threshold.

01:05:37.110 --> 01:05:40.680
So somehow, you have to
sharpen the probabilities.

01:05:40.680 --> 01:05:43.600
You want to say that if the
Jaccard is over the threshold,

01:05:43.600 --> 01:05:45.730
then with almost
like probability 1,

01:05:45.730 --> 01:05:47.230
they're going to
be hashed together.

01:05:47.230 --> 01:05:50.310
And if you below the
threshold, then with basically

01:05:50.310 --> 01:05:53.430
probability zero or very small.

01:05:53.430 --> 01:05:56.700
So how do we do this?

01:05:56.700 --> 01:05:59.160
It's taking kind of
the same trick, which

01:05:59.160 --> 01:06:02.730
is use more hash functions.

01:06:02.730 --> 01:06:05.400
And here, the
construction is you're

01:06:05.400 --> 01:06:09.990
going to break up your n hash
functions into b bands of r hash

01:06:09.990 --> 01:06:10.870
functions.

01:06:10.870 --> 01:06:16.890
So for example, if you have 12
hash functions, and three bands,

01:06:16.890 --> 01:06:20.110
and each band would have
four hash functions.

01:06:20.110 --> 01:06:26.410
So you have H1 through H4, H5
through H8, H9 through H12.

01:06:26.410 --> 01:06:28.780
And then we're just
going to declare

01:06:28.780 --> 01:06:34.840
that A and B are going to
collide if for some band, all

01:06:34.840 --> 01:06:37.040
its hash functions
return the same value.

01:06:37.040 --> 01:06:40.420
So if I have A and
B, I'm going to hash

01:06:40.420 --> 01:06:45.560
using all 12 hash functions for
both A and B using the MinHash.

01:06:45.560 --> 01:06:50.360
And now, I'm going to say
if this band, for example,

01:06:50.360 --> 01:06:52.760
if the hash values
for H1, H2, H3,

01:06:52.760 --> 01:06:55.820
H4 all agree, then
they're going to collide,

01:06:55.820 --> 01:06:59.530
or this band, or this band.

01:06:59.530 --> 01:07:02.980
So there's a and/or
structure, which

01:07:02.980 --> 01:07:06.460
is the key to making
sharpening the probabilities

01:07:06.460 --> 01:07:09.190
around the threshold.

01:07:09.190 --> 01:07:14.080
So now, we just have
to do some analysis

01:07:14.080 --> 01:07:16.945
to calculate what is
the probability that A

01:07:16.945 --> 01:07:20.380
and B collide.

01:07:20.380 --> 01:07:23.410
So similarly, I'm
going to use sym

01:07:23.410 --> 01:07:27.830
to refer to the
Jaccard similarity.

01:07:27.830 --> 01:07:32.130
So OK.

01:07:32.130 --> 01:07:34.370
So I'm going to
say-- let's say I

01:07:34.370 --> 01:07:39.240
have Jaccard similarity of 0.8.

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:42.870
In that case, I'm going
to have five bands.

01:07:42.870 --> 01:07:46.080
Each band has 10 hash functions.

01:07:46.080 --> 01:07:49.670
So a total of 50 hash functions.

01:07:49.670 --> 01:07:56.600
So what is the probability
of a fixed band matching?

01:07:56.600 --> 01:08:02.720
So probably, a fixed band
matching is simply 0.8 to the r.

01:08:02.720 --> 01:08:03.630
Right.

01:08:03.630 --> 01:08:06.680
Because the probability of a
hash or a single hash function

01:08:06.680 --> 01:08:09.530
is just the Jaccard,
which is 0.8.

01:08:09.530 --> 01:08:13.050
And now, what is the probability
of that some band matches?

01:08:13.050 --> 01:08:19.590
Well, it's a probability
that one fixed--

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:24.569
this is the probability that
a fixed band doesn't match.

01:08:24.569 --> 01:08:29.260
And this is the probability
that all of them don't match.

01:08:29.260 --> 01:08:31.560
And then so this is the
probability that at least one

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:32.609
matches.

01:08:32.609 --> 01:08:36.766
So now, you see the probability
of collision is 0.43.

01:08:41.399 --> 01:08:45.300
So it's kind of reshaping
this probability.

01:08:45.300 --> 01:08:48.600
Before, if you have one hash
function if b, and r is 1,

01:08:48.600 --> 01:08:51.990
then the probability would be--

01:08:51.990 --> 01:08:54.490
of collision would be 0.8.

01:08:54.490 --> 01:08:57.029
And now, it's 0.3.

01:08:57.029 --> 01:08:57.670
OK.

01:08:57.670 --> 01:09:01.577
So that's-- but let's--

01:09:01.577 --> 01:09:03.910
I guess here's the picture
you should have in your head.

01:09:03.910 --> 01:09:06.819
So on the x-axis is similarity.

01:09:06.819 --> 01:09:10.080
And what we want is the
mapping from similarity

01:09:10.080 --> 01:09:13.120
to probability of collision
to look something like that.

01:09:13.120 --> 01:09:14.550
If the threshold is--

01:09:14.550 --> 01:09:16.800
the desired threshold
is 0.5, then

01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:20.160
I want everything down here
to be near 0, and everything

01:09:20.160 --> 01:09:21.689
up here to be near 1.

01:09:21.689 --> 01:09:24.369
So let's see what
this looks like.

01:09:24.369 --> 01:09:25.119
I'm going to set--

01:09:29.229 --> 01:09:33.380
for various similarity values,
I'm going to set b equals 10,

01:09:33.380 --> 01:09:34.490
r equals 10.

01:09:34.490 --> 01:09:37.060
And notice that
what this happens

01:09:37.060 --> 01:09:40.850
is that it squashes down some
of these low probabilities,

01:09:40.850 --> 01:09:44.680
and then it sharpens some
of these high probabilities.

01:09:44.680 --> 01:09:46.660
OK.

01:09:46.660 --> 01:09:53.439
But maybe I want to make
this a little bit sharper.

01:09:53.439 --> 01:09:58.630
So what I'm going to do is
to increase r from 10 to 20.

01:09:58.630 --> 01:10:03.580
And what that does is that
moves the curve to the right

01:10:03.580 --> 01:10:06.260
to make it harder to match, and
also sharpens the threshold.

01:10:06.260 --> 01:10:09.550
So if I do that, then
you'll see that now,

01:10:09.550 --> 01:10:10.940
these probabilities go up.

01:10:10.940 --> 01:10:13.520
And now, these probabilities
are much smaller.

01:10:13.520 --> 01:10:19.870
So before, even a
0.7 probability--

01:10:19.870 --> 01:10:23.570
0.7 similarity had
your probability

01:10:23.570 --> 01:10:26.460
of 0.24, which is kind of high.

01:10:26.460 --> 01:10:29.910
I really don't want this,
so I want to squash this.

01:10:29.910 --> 01:10:35.550
So in squashing that, I can
now get it down to 0.007,

01:10:35.550 --> 01:10:38.780
which is pretty good, I guess.

01:10:38.780 --> 01:10:43.560
But now, I've also squashed
some of these probabilities.

01:10:43.560 --> 01:10:47.280
And the goal isn't to shift
everything to the right.

01:10:47.280 --> 01:10:50.600
Otherwise, I might as
well do exact duplication.

01:10:50.600 --> 01:10:52.650
So now, there's another trick.

01:10:52.650 --> 01:10:58.940
If I increase b, which is the
number of buckets or bins,

01:10:58.940 --> 01:11:05.780
then I can essentially
get these probabilities,

01:11:05.780 --> 01:11:09.000
like 0.9 to be in the 90s now.

01:11:09.000 --> 01:11:11.930
And these probabilities
are still fairly low.

01:11:11.930 --> 01:11:15.560
So this picture shows
that as you increase b,

01:11:15.560 --> 01:11:18.500
I get to shift this
curve to the left.

01:11:18.500 --> 01:11:22.090
So increasing r will sharpen
the curve and move things

01:11:22.090 --> 01:11:23.950
to the right, and
then increasing

01:11:23.950 --> 01:11:26.570
b will shift the
curve to the left

01:11:26.570 --> 01:11:30.260
so that you can-- with
appropriate setting of b and r,

01:11:30.260 --> 01:11:33.000
you can get your
really sharp sigmoid.

01:11:35.860 --> 01:11:39.080
OK, so just an example of what
this looks like in practice.

01:11:39.080 --> 01:11:42.460
So there's this paper
that we saw last time

01:11:42.460 --> 01:11:50.920
that uses a near-duplicate--
near deduplication using MinHash

01:11:50.920 --> 01:11:52.420
LSH.

01:11:52.420 --> 01:11:58.572
And their values, b equals
20, and r equals 450.

01:11:58.572 --> 01:12:00.280
So this r is large,
which means that they

01:12:00.280 --> 01:12:03.170
want a really sharp threshold.

01:12:03.170 --> 01:12:07.480
So there's a formula,
which you can compute,

01:12:07.480 --> 01:12:16.960
which says the threshold is
0.99 So that means for every 100

01:12:16.960 --> 01:12:20.030
words, you can only
allow basically one

01:12:20.030 --> 01:12:21.840
word to be different.

01:12:21.840 --> 01:12:26.150
So this is fairly
strict deduplication.

01:12:26.150 --> 01:12:32.960
And the probability that
a fixed band matches

01:12:32.960 --> 01:12:41.100
is this to the r, which is 0.05.

01:12:41.100 --> 01:12:47.130
And then the probability
of collision is 0.64.

01:12:47.130 --> 01:12:49.880
So one thing that's
kind of interesting

01:12:49.880 --> 01:12:53.720
is that I'm computing the
probability of collision

01:12:53.720 --> 01:12:55.050
at the threshold.

01:12:55.050 --> 01:12:59.010
So in some sense, that should
be somewhere in between 0 and 1.

01:12:59.010 --> 01:13:02.030
And it turns out that it
converges to 1 minus 1 over e,

01:13:02.030 --> 01:13:06.080
which is around 0.6-ish.

01:13:06.080 --> 01:13:09.870
So around the threshold,
you could go either way.

01:13:09.870 --> 01:13:12.360
But as soon as you go
above the threshold,

01:13:12.360 --> 01:13:15.720
you have very high probability
of being in a collision.

01:13:15.720 --> 01:13:18.660
And below the threshold, you
have very low probability

01:13:18.660 --> 01:13:21.700
of being in a collision.

01:13:24.962 --> 01:13:25.462
OK.

01:13:28.114 --> 01:13:32.460
So with that, I'm
going to summarize.

01:13:32.460 --> 01:13:35.040
Actually, maybe
I'll ask if there's

01:13:35.040 --> 01:13:38.100
any questions about
deduplication?

01:13:38.100 --> 01:13:43.000
So deduplication makes you run
faster, avoids memorization.

01:13:43.000 --> 01:13:47.140
You can do exact deduplication
with bloom filters,

01:13:47.140 --> 01:13:54.750
or you can use MinHash LSH to
do approximate deduplication.

01:13:54.750 --> 01:13:56.520
Yeah?

01:13:56.520 --> 01:14:00.180
So one question, most of
the duplicated functions

01:14:00.180 --> 01:14:03.190
are based on exact
[INAUDIBLE] hit,

01:14:03.190 --> 01:14:07.210
but if I rewrote an
instance of synthetic data,

01:14:07.210 --> 01:14:08.170
it's like on the rise.

01:14:08.170 --> 01:14:11.110
I was wondering, how do we
duplicate for that use case?

01:14:11.110 --> 01:14:11.610
Yeah.

01:14:11.610 --> 01:14:13.990
So given that synthetic
data is on the rise,

01:14:13.990 --> 01:14:21.160
how do you do duplicate using
something that's, paraphrase

01:14:21.160 --> 01:14:21.770
sensitive.

01:14:21.770 --> 01:14:25.210
So there are some papers,
for example, Sam Pritchett,

01:14:25.210 --> 01:14:29.090
didn't mention, that look
at essentially embedding

01:14:29.090 --> 01:14:33.250
so that can allow
you to get a more

01:14:33.250 --> 01:14:38.710
semantic notion of similarity.

01:14:38.710 --> 01:14:40.930
And this all fits into
the same framework.

01:14:40.930 --> 01:14:44.380
Because in some
sense, LSH was devised

01:14:44.380 --> 01:14:46.340
to find approximate
nearest neighbors.

01:14:46.340 --> 01:14:49.480
And so all you have to
do is have an embedding,

01:14:49.480 --> 01:14:51.850
and that can you embed
all the documents,

01:14:51.850 --> 01:14:55.210
and you can find nearest
neighbors in that space.

01:14:55.210 --> 01:14:57.130
Obviously, there's a
higher cost if you're

01:14:57.130 --> 01:15:01.910
going to run an embedding
to embed your documents.

01:15:01.910 --> 01:15:08.950
And also, we are working in a
regime, where documents are--

01:15:08.950 --> 01:15:14.050
the near duplicates are
really near duplicates.

01:15:14.050 --> 01:15:16.490
I think you have to be very
careful if you're doing

01:15:16.490 --> 01:15:18.750
some more fuzzy duplication.

01:15:18.750 --> 01:15:26.810
You can throw out a lot of your
data if you are too permissive.

01:15:26.810 --> 01:15:27.600
Another question?

01:15:27.600 --> 01:15:29.960
So we know that interpretation
makes all the better.

01:15:29.960 --> 01:15:31.752
If there's any situation
where you actually

01:15:31.752 --> 01:15:33.210
have to duplicate data?

01:15:33.210 --> 01:15:36.420
Since it's so high quality, you
actually want it to be repeated.

01:15:36.420 --> 01:15:36.920
Yeah.

01:15:36.920 --> 01:15:40.710
So the question is, if
the data is high quality,

01:15:40.710 --> 01:15:42.090
maybe you do want duplicates.

01:15:42.090 --> 01:15:44.630
And that's actually right.

01:15:44.630 --> 01:15:47.600
So in a lot of language
modeling papers,

01:15:47.600 --> 01:15:50.570
there's the mid
training regime, where

01:15:50.570 --> 01:15:52.920
you have a bunch of
high quality sources.

01:15:52.920 --> 01:15:54.920
You actually want to
take multiple epochs

01:15:54.920 --> 01:15:57.650
over high quality data.

01:15:57.650 --> 01:16:02.780
So the deduplication is more, I
would say, for the pre-training,

01:16:02.780 --> 01:16:06.050
where you have all this stuff,
and you have 60,000 copies

01:16:06.050 --> 01:16:07.500
of some random thing.

01:16:07.500 --> 01:16:09.480
You just want to clean that up.

01:16:09.480 --> 01:16:15.310
But I think the optimal
thing to do is--

01:16:15.310 --> 01:16:16.192
well, clearly not.

01:16:16.192 --> 01:16:18.150
I don't know what the
optimal thing to do this.

01:16:18.150 --> 01:16:22.950
But it's probably--
if you have documents

01:16:22.950 --> 01:16:25.080
that occur a lot of
times, you could also

01:16:25.080 --> 01:16:27.610
signal that there's
more importance to it.

01:16:27.610 --> 01:16:29.700
And maybe the right
thing to do is

01:16:29.700 --> 01:16:31.370
taking the number of the count.

01:16:31.370 --> 01:16:33.120
And, I don't know,
taking the square root,

01:16:33.120 --> 01:16:36.720
or log, or something
so that it shows up--

01:16:36.720 --> 01:16:39.550
doesn't proportionally show
up in your training data,

01:16:39.550 --> 01:16:43.320
but you acknowledge that
it's important enough

01:16:43.320 --> 01:16:45.380
to go take more epochs over it.

01:16:49.230 --> 01:16:51.900
So let's summarize.

01:16:51.900 --> 01:16:55.330
So this lecture was mostly about
giving you algorithmic tools.

01:16:55.330 --> 01:16:59.710
So we looked at n-gram
models, linear classifiers,

01:16:59.710 --> 01:17:03.700
and importance sampling
as ways to do filtering.

01:17:03.700 --> 01:17:08.460
And with filtering, you can do
language identification, quality

01:17:08.460 --> 01:17:12.670
filtering, toxicity
filtering, anything, I guess,

01:17:12.670 --> 01:17:16.700
where you have a
targets data set.

01:17:16.700 --> 01:17:18.930
And you want to get
more of that data set,

01:17:18.930 --> 01:17:20.820
then you can apply these tools.

01:17:20.820 --> 01:17:22.850
Or if you don't have
a target data set,

01:17:22.850 --> 01:17:25.620
and you have a strong
language models,

01:17:25.620 --> 01:17:27.620
you can prompt
that language model

01:17:27.620 --> 01:17:34.520
to synthesize a, or synthesize
or filter down a lower quality

01:17:34.520 --> 01:17:39.350
data set to get to T, and then
you get a fast classifier that--

01:17:39.350 --> 01:17:41.420
and you're off to the races.

01:17:41.420 --> 01:17:44.460
And then second, we
looked at deduplication,

01:17:44.460 --> 01:17:51.530
which is important for
reducing the amount of compute

01:17:51.530 --> 01:17:53.660
you're spending on--

01:17:53.660 --> 01:17:55.940
you're wasting, essentially.

01:17:55.940 --> 01:18:00.980
And the algorithmic
tool here is hashing.

01:18:00.980 --> 01:18:02.540
Because hashing
is what allows you

01:18:02.540 --> 01:18:05.660
to take these kind of
pairwise notions of similarity

01:18:05.660 --> 01:18:09.000
and collision and turn
them into unary functions,

01:18:09.000 --> 01:18:12.880
which allows you to have
this linear time property.

01:18:12.880 --> 01:18:16.530
And we saw some
of clever methods

01:18:16.530 --> 01:18:20.460
where basically using
multiple hash functions

01:18:20.460 --> 01:18:22.500
and using these kind
of and/or constructions

01:18:22.500 --> 01:18:28.650
allows you to shape your
criteria in ways like the LSH

01:18:28.650 --> 01:18:29.910
example.

01:18:29.910 --> 01:18:33.900
So now, you have all the tools.

01:18:33.900 --> 01:18:37.660
Now, in some sense, if you
ask how you teach data,

01:18:37.660 --> 01:18:40.800
this is only the beginning.

01:18:40.800 --> 01:18:43.870
You really have to spend time
with the data, looking at it,

01:18:43.870 --> 01:18:45.280
filtering, and training models.

01:18:45.280 --> 01:18:49.590
And that's how you build your
intuitions over what works

01:18:49.590 --> 01:18:50.410
and what doesn't.

01:18:50.410 --> 01:18:52.920
So hopefully, you'll be
able to do that a bit

01:18:52.920 --> 01:18:55.470
in assignment four.

01:18:55.470 --> 01:18:56.150
OK.

01:18:56.150 --> 01:18:58.600
That's all for the data lecture.

01:18:58.600 --> 01:19:00.630
And next week,
we're going to start

01:19:00.630 --> 01:19:04.320
doing reinforcement learning
and alignment, which

01:19:04.320 --> 01:19:06.590
is will be our last unit.
